\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee marginally higher, but wary of c.bank  \b0\par\par\uc2 346 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Februar 2006\par 04:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates to open)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Feb 14 (Reuters) - The rupee opened marginally higher on Tuesday on expectations of foreign fund flows into the booming stock market, but traders remained wary of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 A dip in the dollar against the yen <JPY=> also supported the rupee, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 At 09:40 a.m. (0410 GMT), the rupee <INR=IN> was quoted at 44.22/23 per dollar, 0.09 percent firmer than Monday's close.  \par \par \uc2 "Foreign fund inflows are strong and that's pushing up the rupee early," said the chief trader at an Indian bank. "But we expect \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap gains at 44.20."  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's benchmark stock index <.BSESN> hit a fresh high on Monday. Foreign funds bought net shares worth $138 million on Friday, taking the total to about $1.5 billion so far this year.  \par \par \uc2 While this has put upward pressure on the rupee, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains have been capped by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Indian state-run banks, acting on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not want rupee strength to hurt exporters' competitiveness and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market has helped weaken the rupee 0.42 percent from four-month highs hit two weeks ago.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said they suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Market attention was also turning to new U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke, who testifies to the House Financial Services Committee on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Bernanke is expected to reinforce a perception in the market that the Fed will keep raising interest rates in the months ahead.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  lifted rates by 25 basis points to 4.5 percent last month, the 14th straight increase since mid-2004.  \par \par \uc2 Further U.S. rate increases could tempt rate-sensitive foreign funds away from emerging markets like India.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  ------------------------------------------------------------\par                  MARKET SNAPSHOT\par  Bombay Sensitive Index          <.BSESN>\par  Indian rupee (/$)               <INR=IN>\par  ------------------------------------------------------------\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|SNS|SNI|ABN|M|RBN|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060217e22e0009z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
