\par\par \b \uc2 Indian cbank curtails rupee gains, dollar eyed  \b0\par\par\uc2 427 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Februar 2006\par 03:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates to close)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Feb 3 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the second straight session on Friday, absorbing most of the dollar inflows from foreign funds investing in local stocks and keeping the rupee on a leash, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  might have been done with an eye on easing tight liquidity in the banking system ahead of the borrowing of 60 billion rupees by the government early next week.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the rupee might give up some strength on Monday if the dollar kept gains against the yen and the euro after the release of key U.S. jobs data later on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the partially convertible rupee <INR=IN> made modest gains to end at 44.1950/2050 per dollar, up 0.13 percent from Thursday's close of 44.2550/2650.  \par \par \uc2 The rupee hit a four-month intra-day high around 44.0425 on Tuesday but quickly retreated as the dollar's rise and defence-related import payments weighed it down.  \par \par \uc2 Dollar purchases by state-run banks also pushed down the rupee on Thursday. State banks often buy dollars on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  through them when it wants to calm \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility.  \par \par \uc2 "The dollar purchases by the state-run banks which we saw yesterday continued," said a trader at a government-run bank. "The rupee would have gained much more but for their purchases. But the rupee could ease a bit on Monday if the dollar gains overseas."  \par \par \uc2 The dollar hit a seven-week peak against the yen <JPY=> as the market held fire before the jobs report, which could provide clues on whether the U.S. Federal Reserve will continue to push up interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 Since the U.S. Federal Reserve began a campaign of rate rises in 2004, the interest rate gap between the United States and India has shrunk to 100 basis points from 350, with U.S. rates at 4.5 percent and India's at 5.5 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Many in the market say that if the gap narrows further it could hurt the rupee since the Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is supported by foreign capital in search of extra yield.  \par \par \uc2 India's share index has risen nearly 3.8 percent in 2006 on the back of foreign portfolio inflows of $816 million.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  ------------------------------------------------------------\par                                MARKET SNAPSHOT\par  Bombay Sensitive Index          <.BSESN>\par  Indian rupee (/$)               <INR=IN>\par  ------------------------------------------------------------\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|Table|SNS|SNI|ABN|M|RBN|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060206e2230004a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
