\par\par \b \uc2 India rupee at near-15-mo low on arbitrage dlr buys  \b0\par\par\uc2 498 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Dezember 2005\par 03:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates to close)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Dec 2 (Reuters) - The rupee fell to a near-15-month closing low on Friday, weakening 0.4 percent, after foreign banks and investors bought dollars to capitalise on an arbitrage against costlier offshore forwards.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the dollar's steady climb against the yen to a 32-month high, an afternoon slide off highs by Indian stocks and the absence of sustained \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had also contributed to the weak sentiment on the rupee.  \par \par \uc2 The partly convertible rupee <INR=IN> suffered its biggest percentage fall in nearly a month to end at 46.18/19 per dollar. It previously closed lower at 46.265/270 on Sept. 10, 2004.  \par \par \uc2 But the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which closed at 45.995/46.005 on Thursday, was off a session low of 46.24, helped by long dollar unwinding near close as some banks opted for caution ahead of the weekend.  \par \par \uc2 "The NDF(non-deliverable forward)-related (dollar) buying started the rupee's fall initially," a chief dealer at a foreign bank said.  \par \par \uc2 "With the RBI (Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) only trying to smoothen the move rather than \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a sustained manner, the yen losing more ground against the dollar and stocks retreating in the afternoon, there were really no positives for the rupee."  \par \par \uc2 The opportunity to gain more than 0.10-0.15 rupees by selling dollar-rupee in the one- to three-month segment in the NDF market <PNDF> had fuelled demand for the strengthening U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the morning.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which had briefly supported the rupee early with a verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  leading to dollar selling by state-run banks, had probably refrained from stronger action on concerns about draining rupees.  \par \par \uc2 Liquidity in the banking system fell sharply last month, pushing up overnight borrowing costs and traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have been keen to ensure that cash surpluses remain ample ahead of an 80-billion-rupee federal bond sale on Dec. 6.  \par \par \uc2 The rupee has lost nearly 6 percent in 2005 on growing worries about a widening trade deficit -- forecast to hit a record $48 billion in March -- and the dollar's gains overseas.  \par \par \uc2 Only robust foreign fund inflows of more than $8.6 billion into Indian stocks this year have given some relief to the rupee.  \par \par \uc2 But with the main Mumbai stock index <.BSESN> sliding off a new high on Friday to end just 0.19 percent up, in a market where losing issues outnumbered gainers, the rupee's outlook remained weak, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 "Unless the dollar's trend reverses overseas, we should still see the rupee under pressure," a dealer at a state-run bank said.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ------------------------------------------------------------\par                                MARKET SNAPSHOT\par  Bombay Sensitive Index          <.BSESN>\par  Indian rupee (/$)               <INR=IN>\par  7.38 percent 2015 bond (%)      <IN073815G=>\par   ------------------------------------------------------------\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|RBN|SNS|SNI|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020051202e1c20005s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
