\par\par \b \uc2 India Rupee Falls But Ends Off Lows; Bonds Up  \b0\par\par\uc2 901 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 November 2005\par 14:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee extended losses against the U.S. dollar for the second straight session Monday but ended off the day's lows as state-run banks sold dollars, possibly on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's behalf.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier in the session, the rupee slid, unnerved by the dollar's rise against other global majors and month-end demand for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from oil importing companies, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar ended at INR45.96, just shy of the psychologically crucial INR46.00 mark, although above Friday's INR45.82.  \par \par \uc2 "A large state-owned bank sold dollars at 45.9850, then bought around 46.0000 and again sold around 45.9700," said a foreign bank dealer. "It's difficult to say whether the move was a trading punt or \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but it appears the second possibility is stronger, as several state-run banks sold all at once."  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  usually \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  through state-run banks to curb excessive volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2 Some market watchers believe the rupee's weakness is "an automatic response" to India's widening current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 "The fall was imminent, considering strong import growth in recent months coupled with high global oil prices," said Raja Bandhyopadhyay, analyst at Debtonnet.com.  \par \par \uc2 India's current account deficit ballooned to $6.20 billion during April-June from a $3.39 billion surplus in the same year-ago period. Economists said rising investment demand in a growing economy is expected to push the trade gap further into the red.  \par \par \uc2 Tarini Vaidya, country treasurer at private-sector Centurion Bank, believes the rupee's weakening could be sustained on account of strong demand for oil. "If this trend continues, dollar-rupee could soon test 46.25," she said.  \par \par \uc2 Indian government bonds rose Monday as investors cheered the introduction of an additional daily liquidity adjustment facility on hopes it will improve system cash.  \par \par \uc2 "The new system will help ensure the call money rate doesn't go through the roof whenever liquidity is a bit tight," said a private sector bank dealer. "It will aid better liquidity management."  \par \par \uc2 After close of trade Friday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would conduct the repurchase and reverse repurchase auctions twice daily from Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the move would help stabilize the overnight call money rate, which had risen to nearly 7.0% earlier this month from the usual 5.25%-5.50% when outflows toward bond sales and stronger demand for loans caused a cash crunch.  \par \par \uc2 The actively traded 7.49% bond maturing in 2017 closed at INR102.10, up from its previous close of INR101.91. Its yield fell three basis points to 7.21%. The benchmark 10-year bond's yield slipped two basis points to 7.06%.  \par \par \uc2 Underlying sentiment was slightly cautious as market participants await fresh bond issues due early next month. As part of its regular market borrowing, the government is set to raise INR80 billion between Dec. 1-8.  \par \par \uc2 "Some profit booking came in toward the close of trade. Although the broad trend will be for a rise, the upside will be limited, at least until the auction announcement," said a trader at a primary dealing firm.  \par \par \uc2 The overnight interbank call money rate ended up at 5.30%-5.40% from 4.00%-5.00% as banks upped demand for funds at the start of a new reporting fortnight.  \par \par \uc2 Local banks typically report their cash reserve positions over two weeks to the RBI - banks are required to maintain reserves equivalent to 5% of net liabilities and report such positions to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on alternate Fridays.  \par \par \uc2 Dollar forward rates ended lower in lackluster trade. The dollar forward contract maturing in October 2006 closed down at a premium of INR0.2150 from INR0.2200 previously to the spot rate.  \par \par \uc2 Interest rate swaps benchmarked to the Mumbai interbank forward offered rate, or Mifor, closed little changed. The two-year swap rate ended marginally up at 5.47%-5.52% from 5.46%-5.51%, while the five-year rate ended at 5.85%-5.90% from 5.83%-5.88% at the previous close.  \par \par \uc2 Overnight indexed swap rates ended mostly flat. The five-year swap closed at 6.35%-6.36% from 6.34%-6.36% at the previous close. [ 28-11-05 1315GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Rupee Vs Dollar Close \par Monday        Previous \par INR45.9600    INR45.8200 \par  \par Forward Dollar Premiums/Discounts \par January                 April                   October \par INR0.0675 Vs INR0.0725  INR0.1200 vs INR0.1275  INR0.2150 Vs INR0.2200 \par  \par Daily Overnight Reverse Repurchase Auction Amount \par Monday        Previous \par INR62.45 bln  INR36.85 bln \par  \par Reverse Repurchase Auction Rate \par Monday        Previous \par 5.25%         5.25% \par  \par Call Rate \par Monday        Previous \par 5.30%-5.40%   4.00%-5.00% \par  \par Interest Rate Swaps  Current Price   Previous Price \par  2-Year Mifor swap   5.47%-5.52%       5.46%-5.51% \par  5-Year Mifor swap   5.85%-5.90%       5.83%-5.88% \par  5-Year OIS          6.35%-6.36%       6.34%-6.36% \par  \par Benchmark Bond Prices \par Security   Maturity   Current Price   Previous Price \par  7.27% GOI   2013        102.17