\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: India's Sensex At New Milestone; Rupee Stumbles  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Anil Varma  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 892 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 November 2005\par 14:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones Asian Equities Report\par \uc2 AER\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--Bullish foreign investors pushed Indian shares to a fresh record high Monday but the rupee fell as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market ignored the capital inflows to fret over the country's current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 The key Indian share index, the Bombay Stock Exchange's 30-share Sensex, broke the 9000-point barrier for the first time in its 130-year history in Monday's intraday trading but slipped back later on profit-taking.  \par \par \uc2 It still ended the day at a record-high 8,994.94 points, up 1.2%, or 105.91 points, from Friday. The index has gained 49% in the past 12 months and 41% since the current fiscal year began April 1.  \par \par \uc2 The 9000-point mark is the third major milestone the index has crossed in 2005, after passing the 8000 level in September and the 7000 level in June.  \par \par \uc2 Market-watchers say many more are to come in the years ahead.  \par \par \uc2 The 9000 mark "is one of many more milestones we are likely to see in the likely bull run over the next few years," said Jawahir Mulraj, India representative at U.S.-based publication Institutional Investor.  \par \par \uc2 "Sensex should be heading toward 15,000 over the next two to three years," as the country's strong economy continues to attract capital, he said.  \par \par \uc2 Being Asia's fastest-growing economy after China, has kept India an attractive destination for investors, even though some investment houses have started saying local assets are too expensive.  \par \par \uc2 Official forecasts peg the country's economic expansion in the current fiscal year at 7.0%-7.5%, up from 6.9% in fiscal 2004-2005.  \par \par \uc2 The strong economy and the stock-market rally have attracted foreign investment worth $8.49 billion in Indian shares so far in 2005. This is just shy of the record $8.51 billion foreigners invested in 2004.  \par \par \uc2 However, some are worried the party has lasted too long.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier in November Merrill Lynch downgraded India's equities rating to underweight, citing rising inflation and interest rates, falling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and expensive valuations.  \par \par \uc2 Merrill also noted Sensex valuations are the most expensive among all emerging countries at 14.4 times earnings for the current financial year, compared with 13.6 times for Malaysia, 11.5 times for Taiwan, 9.5 times for Thailand, 9 times for Indonesia and 8.9 times for South Korea.  \par \par \uc2 Indeed, even as the long-term prospects for Indian equities remain positive, the near-term outlook is one of caution.  \par \par \uc2 Ajit Sangvi, director at Mumbai-based MSS Securities, noted the latest rise in local stocks hasn't been as broad-based as past rallies, and was characterized by the sizable gains of a handful of heavyweights.  \par \par \uc2 Monday's rally was led by blue chips such as Oil & Natural Gas Corp., consumer goods major Hindustan Lever and technology firm Wipro.  \par \par \uc2 Sangvi said the trend in local shares may depend increasingly on liquidity in the financial system in the coming weeks.  \par \par \uc2 He expects the Sensex to remain in a 8850-9250 range in the near term.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Rupee Falls \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The rupee fell sharply against the dollar Monday, briefly slipping below key psychological support at INR46 a dollar as banks and companies bought a globally strong U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . It later recovered partly on suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central-bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar ended at INR45.96, sharply up from INR45.82 at the previous close but below an intraday high of INR46.02.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Raja Bandhyopadhyay, analyst at Debtonnet.com, said the rupee's fall is "an automatic response" to the country's widening current account deficit. India's current account showed a $6.20 billion deficit in the April-June quarter, compared with a $3.39 billion surplus in the year-ago period.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With the current account deficit set to widen more, the downward pressure on the rupee may only increase.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Despite the recent evidence of a recovery in equity flows, we maintain a bearish rupee view. Even if the capital account posts a surplus ... the overall balance of payments will be down substantially owing to a wider current account deficit," Siddharth Mathur, strategist at JPMorgan India, said in a recent research report.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan recently revised upward its forecast of the Indian current account deficit for the current fiscal year to 2.2% of the country's gross domestic product from 1.3% earlier.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It forecast the dollar at INR46.50 by the end of March 2006 and INR47.50 by the end of December 2006.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The rupee's trade-weighted overvaluation also may work against it. The rupee is overvalued by more than 8%, according to analysts.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Market-watchers believe although the Reserve Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market to smooth out undue volatility, it will tolerate a gradual rupee decline in the coming months, as that could help correct the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's trade-weighted overvaluation and help make Indian exports more competitive in the global markets.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Indian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  doesn't fix any target level or range for the rupee, but often \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market to smooth market volatility or to prevent the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from straying too far from fair valuation.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Anil Varma, Dow Jones Newswires; 91 22 22884212; anil.varma@dowjones.com [ 28-11-05 1306GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument AER0000020051128e1bs00033\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
