\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-Indian rupee seen testing 46/dollar, cbank absent  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Annapurni Hariharan and Suresh Seshadri  \par \uc2 555 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 November 2005\par 03:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Repeats to local subscribers)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Nov 7 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee is likely to test 46.00-46.50 per dollar <INR=IN> shortly in the absence of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  support after falling 0.8 percent on Monday to a 13-month low of 45.9200, traders say.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to calm volatility on the exchange rate but dealers say it has not stepped in to buy rupees since Oct. 18, when it did so briefly through state-run banks to stop the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  falling too acutely.  \par \par \uc2 Since then the rupee has shed 1.6 percent. Dealers expect a test of 46.00 or even 46.50 over the next day or two, levels untouched since September last year, before the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  steps in to support it.  \par \par \uc2 "They vehemently defended that level last year and so we expect 46.50 to be a strong support level for the rupee," a chief dealer at a state-run bank said.  \par \par \uc2 The rupee closed at 45.91/92 per dollar and other traders also said they did not expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to act before 46.00.  \par \par \uc2 The government auctions 80 billion rupees ($1.74 billion) worth of debt on Tuesday and traders said the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not want to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and drain rupees from the system beforehand as this would diminish cash surpluses which would normally be used to buy bonds.  \par \par \uc2 "We shouldn't see the RBI for sometime now as we have very thin liquidity in the system and the auction could totally suck out liquidity," said R.K. Gurumurthy, acting Treasury head at ING Vysysa Bank in Bangalore.  \par \par \uc2 "I have only a resistance at 46.05 and the next 46.50. We could test these most likely this week," he said.  \par \par \uc2 The 46.05 level is a 50 percent retracement of the move to a 6-year high of 43.01 set in July this year from its all-time low in May 2002 which he charted at 49.09. The 46.50 level is a previous big dollar/rupee top of August 2004.  \par \par \uc2 The rupee has fallen as a wealth of foreign fund inflows in the first nine months of this year dried up and began to reverse in October on the back of a sharp stock market correction.  \par \par \uc2 India's current account has turned into deficit this year and, without the fund inflows, that gap has exerted downward pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In addition, Indian importers, nervous that the dollar is on the rise as a result of higher U.S. interest rates, have been buying up the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Traders said there was another reason why the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had not stepped in to curb weakness up to this point.  \par \par \uc2 "Despite the fact that the rupee is depreciating against the dollar, it has gained against the euro and sterling, which means that it is still overvalued," said a chief dealer at a state-run bank.  \par \par \uc2 JP Morgan's real effective exchange rate <JPMREER>, which is similar to that of the RBI, shows the rupee is 7.5 percent overvalued compared with just 1.7 percent a year ago.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE-OUTLOOK (REPEAT)|LANGEN|SNS|SNI|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020051108e1b800057\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
