\par\par \b \uc2 Indian Rupee At Fresh 11-Mo Low On Outflows, Trade Gap  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,101 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Oktober 2005\par 16:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee broke key psychological supports to end at a fresh 11-month low Tuesday as a widening trade gap and a negative stock market trend fueled concerns about portfolio outflows.  \par \par \uc2 Widening differentials between the rupee market and the overseas non-deliverable forward rate combined with the dollar's broad rally overseas also weighed on the domestic unit, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar broke through the key INR45.00 resistance level to end up at INR45.1650 from INR44.8800 at the last close. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has appreciated 3.3% from April 1, when India's current fiscal year started, gaining almost 2.7% so far this month alone amid signs of a slowdown in stock-related flows into local shares.  \par \par \uc2 Foreigners have sold local stocks worth $228.9 million so far this month, compared with purchases of $971.7 million in September and $1.014 billion in August.  \par \par \uc2 Although some expect the rupee to weaken more during the coming weeks due to a widening trade deficit, they believe the trend may not attract Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the weakness "is a part of a global trend," said Sreebhashyam Srinivas, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at Nova Scotia Bank in Mumbai. He expects the rupee to test INR45.50 by December.  \par \par \uc2 The Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  typically \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth market volatility or prevent the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from straying from its fair valuation. In trade-weighted terms, the rupee is currently more than 8% overvalued.  \par \par \uc2 Latest data show India's current account deficit in the April-June quarter was $6.20 billion compared with a $3.39 billion surplus in the same period of 2004.  \par \par \uc2 Widening differentials between the non-deliverable forward and the onshore market have also pressured the rupee down in recent sessions.  \par \par \uc2 "The differential between the onshore dollar/rupee and the one-year forward in the non-deliverable forwards market is huge, nearly INR0.21, and it has had a domino effect," said a dealer at a private bank.  \par \par \uc2 NDFs are offshore financial instruments used by foreign investors to bet on the value of the rupee. A cheaper offshore rupee prompts foreign investors to buy dollars in the local market and sell them overseas, thus opening an opportunity to arbitrage between the two markets.  \par \par \uc2 In the debt market, government bonds ended lower as a sharp fall in the rupee reinforced worries the RBI would likely hike the reverse repurchase rate by 25 basis points at its Oct. 25 policy review.  \par \par \uc2 Volumes were modest, with few investors willing to build fresh positions ahead of next week's policy statement.  \par \par \uc2 The popular 10.25% 2021 bond ended down at INR125.07 from INR125.32 at the last close. Its yield rose to 7.50% from 7.47%. The yield on the 10-year benchmark, 7.38% bond was higher at 7.16% from 7.15%.  \par \par \uc2 "A sharp drop in the rupee below critical resistance levels (likely 45.20) could set in motion monetary forces. A rate hike seems likely," said an ABN AMRO report.  \par \par \uc2 Most market participants expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise key short-term interest rates to contain price pressures caused by strong domestic demand and high oil prices.  \par \par \uc2 Data show wholesale-price-based inflation rose 4.24% during the week ended Oct. 1. Although inflation had eased to three-year low of 3.01% in August, economists say a local oil price hike and a waning statistical base effect could push it up to around 5.0%-5.5% by March.  \par \par \uc2 "There are also concerns liquidity may not be very comfortable for long," said a state-run bank dealer.  \par \par \uc2 "A lot depends on how the redemption of the India Millennium Deposits is handled," said K. Harihar, treasurer at Development Credit Bank. The State Bank of India's $5.5 billion, five-year IMD is due to mature in December, possibly the country's single-biggest capital outflow this year.  \par \par \uc2 Money market liquidity - as indicated by bids at the RBI's daily reverse repo auction - has ebbed in recent sessions on the back of a pullout by foreign funds from domestic stock markets and the RBI's recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when it sold dollars to cushion the domestic unit's slide.  \par \par \uc2 The RBI accepted average daily bids worth INR202 billion so far in October compared with INR305 billion and INR356 billion in September and August respectively.  \par \par \uc2 The overnight call money rate closed unchanged at 5.0%-5.1% with banks' demand for funds modest amid dull trade.  \par \par \uc2 "Markets were mostly quiet today, the rate hovered around 5.0% for most of the session," said a trader at a primary dealing firm.  \par \par \uc2 In the derivatives segment, dollar forward rates fell Tuesday, as banks and exporters sold dollars in a mild bout of profit taking. The dollar forward contract maturing in September 2006 closed down at a premium of INR0.1800 to the spot rate from INR0.2050 previously.  \par \par \uc2 Near-term interest rate swaps benchmarked to the Mumbai interbank offered rate, or Mifor, closed marginally down, in line with the slip in dollar forward rates. The two-year swap rate ended lower at 5.32%-5.36% from 5.34%-5.38% while the five-year rate closed marginally up at 5.80%-5.84% from 5.79%-5.83%.  \par \par \uc2 Overnight indexed swap rates inched up, in line with a rise in government bonds yields. The 5-year swap ended higher up at 6.39%-6.41% from 6.37%-6.39% at the previous close. [ 18-10-05 1408GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Rupee Vs Dollar Close \par Tuesday        Previous \par INR45.1650     INR44.8800 \par  \par Forward Dollar Premiums/Discounts \par December                 March                  September \par INR0.0400 Vs INR0.0500   INR0.085 Vs INR0.100   INR0.180 Vs INR0.205 \par  \par Daily Overnight Reverse Repurchase Auction Amount \par Tuesday        Previous \par INR190.00 bln  INR172.05 bln \par  \par Reverse Repurchase Auction Rate \par Tuesday        Previous \par 5.00%          5.00% \par  \par Call Rate \par Tuesday        Previous Close \par 5.00%-5.10%    5.00%-5.10% \par  \par Interest Rate Swaps  Current Price   Previous Price \par  2-Year Mifor swap   5.32%-5.36%       5.34%-5.38% \par  5-Year Mifor swap   5.80%-5.84%       5.79%-5.83% \par  5-Year OIS          6.39%-6.41%       6.37%-6.39% \par  \par Benchmark Bond Prices \par Security   Maturity   Current Price   Previous Price \par  7.27% GOI   2013        101.70          101.80 \par  7.37% GOI   2014        102.05          102.21 \par 10.25% GOI   2021        125.07          125.32 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Judith D'Souza, Dow Jones Newswires; 91 22 22884212; judith.dsouza@dowjones.com [ 18-10-05 1408GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1164 1847 62506  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020051018e1ai000qf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
