\par\par \b \uc2 India Rupee At Fresh 10-Mo Low; Weak Bias To Persist  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,164 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Oktober 2005\par 15:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee ended at a new 10-month low against the U.S. dollar Monday as a widening trade deficit and the dollar's firm trend overseas triggered heavy dollar purchases by oil companies.  \par \par \uc2 Slowing portfolio investments and increasing differentials between non-deliverable rupee forwards and the onshore market also weighed on the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar closed at INR44.7550, up from INR44.3800 at the previous close. It last reached a similar level of INR44.7900 Dec. 10.  \par \par \uc2 The rupee has weakened some 1.5% over the past two weeks but traders say the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the rupee as the domestic unit is still overvalued in trade-weighted terms.  \par \par \uc2 "Other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have also been weakening, and moreover, the rupee is 10% overvalued in trade-weighted terms," said a private sector bank trader. "I personally don't think the RBI will step in."  \par \par \uc2 He expects the dollar to trade between INR44.75 and INR44.80 this week, buoyed by continued dollar demand from oil companies.  \par \par \uc2 Sentiment towards the rupee has been hit in recent sessions by signs of falling portfolio investment in Indian shares, pressuring a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  already weighed down by a widening trade gap and high global oil prices.  \par \par \uc2 "The general theme has been that of rupee weakness; there is a case for the dollar to rise to 45 by December," said Monish Tahilramani, head of treasury at HSBC, India.  \par \par \uc2 The latest data from the Securities and Exchange Board of India show that foreigners sold shares worth $56 million over the past five sessions compared with purchases worth $17.4 million.  \par \par \uc2 "Until now, the rising trade deficit was cushioned by portfolio inflows, but now with some slowdown on that front, the rupee looks set to weaken," said S.P. Prabhu, fixed-income analyst at IDBI Capital Markets.  \par \par \uc2 Prabhu expects India's trade deficit to widen to $50 billion by the end of the current fiscal year in March 2006 from $38 billion last year. This could contribute to the rupee's weakening bias, taking the dollar to INR45 by March, he said.  \par \par \uc2 Some \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market participants say the widening differential between non-deliverable rupee forwards and onshore markets is also weighing on the local unit.  \par \par \uc2 "One-month differentials between NDF and onshore markets were around INR0.15 last, which is quite attractive," said a local dealer.  \par \par \uc2 NDFs are offshore financial instruments used by foreign investors to bet on the value of the rupee. The rupee is typically cheaper against the dollar in the offshore market, prompting foreign investors to buy dollars in the local market and sell them overseas for a better price.  \par \par \uc2 Indian government bonds ended lower Friday, as a fall in the rupee "spooked sentiment" and most investors remained sidelined ahead of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Oct. 25 monetary policy review, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 "It's still a 50-50 call on what the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is going to do this policy review and the market is trading in a narrow INR0.10-0.15 range," said a local dealer.  \par \par \uc2 Although some segments of the market expect a 25-basis-point hike in the reverse repurchase rate to contain inflationary pressures, others believe there is little reason for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise rates given that the domestic economy is strong but not overheating.  \par \par \uc2 The latest data show the Indian economy grew by 8.1% during April-June, the first quarter of the current fiscal year, after expanding by 7.6% in the year-ago period.  \par \par \uc2 But inflation still poses a threat. Although the rate eased to three-year low of 3.01% in August, analysts say a local oil price hike and a waning statistical base-effect could push it up to around 5.0%-5.5% by March. The latest year-on-year inflation was 3.97% in the week to Sept. 24.  \par \par \uc2 The liquid 10.25% 2021 bond ended down at INR125.10 from INR125.23 at the previous close. Its yield edged up to 7.50% from 7.48% previously. The yield on the benchmark 10-year, 7.38% bond ended up at 7.18% from 7.16%.  \par \par \uc2 In the market for overnight funds, the call money rate closed flat at 4.95%-5.05%, with banks' demand for funds to meet mandatory cash reserve targets offset by adequate system liquidity.  \par \par \uc2 Money market liquidity has been ample in recent sessions, as indicated by bids at a recent RBI reverse repurchase auction - the lowest yielding money market instrument. The RBI accepted daily average bids worth INR244 billion last week, above INR224 billion accepted in the week prior.  \par \par \uc2 But "if the rupee continues to fall sharply, then the RBI might step in to stem excessive volatility and that may pressure liquidity," said a trader at a primary dealing firm.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers are concerned the RBI could buy rupees and sell dollars to support the local unit, thus sucking out liquidity from the money market.  \par \par \uc2 In the derivatives segment, dollar forward rates fell Monday as the sharp uptick in the dollar-rupee in recent sessions prompted some banks to enter into buy-spot-sell-forward swaps on concerns about a cash USD shortage. The dollar forward contract, maturing in September 2006, closed down at a premium of INR0.2550 to the spot rate from INR0.2925 previously.  \par \par \uc2 The interest rate swaps benchmarked to the Mumbai interbank offered rate, or MIFOR, ended lower, tracking dollar forward rates. The two-year swap rate ended down at 5.40%-5.45% from 5.42%-5.46% last, while the five-year rate closed lower at 5.80%-5.84% from 5.82%-5.87%.  \par \par \uc2 Overnight indexed swap rates ended marginally higher Friday, in line with government bond yields. The 5-year swap ended up at 6.38%-6.39% from 6.37%-6.38% at the previous close. [ 10-10-05 1316GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Rupee Vs Dollar Close \par Monday         Previous \par INR44.7550     INR44.3800 \par  \par Forward Dollar Premiums/Discounts \par December               March                  September \par INR0.080 Vs INR0.100   INR0.140 Vs INR0.160   INR0.2550 Vs INR0.2925 \par  \par Daily Overnight Reverse Repurchase Auction Amount \par Monday         Previous \par INR180.50 bln  INR215.10 bln \par  \par Reverse Repurchase Auction Rate \par Monday         Previous \par 5.00%          5.00% \par  \par Call Rate \par Monday         Previous Close \par 4.95%-5.05%    4.95%-5.05% \par  \par Interest Rate Swaps  Current Price   Previous Price \par  2-Year Mifor swap   5.40%-5.45%       5.42%-5.46% \par  5-Year Mifor swap   5.80%-5.84%       5.82%-5.87% \par  5-Year OIS          6.38%-6.39%       6.37%-6.38% \par  \par Benchmark Bond Prices \par Security   Maturity   Current Price   Previous Price \par  7.27% GOI   2013        101.72          101.75 \par  7.37% GOI   2014        102.08          102.08 \par 10.25% GOI   2021        125.10          125.23 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Judith D'Souza, Dow Jones Newswires; 91 22 22884212; judith.dsouza@dowjones.com [ 10-10-05 1317GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1164 1847 62506  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020051010e1aa000kd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley