\par\par \b \uc2 RBI WATCH: Rupee Support Favored To Beat Inflation  \b0\par\par\uc2 920 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 September 2005\par 10:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Anil Varma \par  \par    A Dow Jones Newswires Column \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--The Reserve Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets last week to stem the rupee's fall against the dollar indicates the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is worried that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's fall will exacerbate growing inflation pressures.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Despite helping engineer six weeks ago the rupee's fall from its recent peak through heavy dollar purchases, the RBI appears to have decided that enough is enough.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rupee purchases last week have helped the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  recover from a near nine-month low against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts say that the RBI, keen to keep a lid on inflation, will use its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to offset mounting downward pressure on the rupee due to a widening current account gap and a falling capital account surplus until the outlook for domestic prices is more stable.  \par \par \uc2 They say that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is more likely to focus on supporting the rupee, rather than raise interest rates, in its fight against inflation.  \par \par \uc2 "With global oil prices continuing their relentless upward march, the desire to limit the local impact of higher international oil prices and to keep inflationary pressures in check will keep the pressure on RBI to restrain any (downward) correction in the rupee," said Siddharth Mathur, strategist at JPMorgan India, in a recent research report.  \par \par \uc2 The RBI sold dollars in the spot market last week after the dollar hit INR44.15, the greenback's highest level since Dec. 15, 2004.  \par \par \uc2 Not many weeks prior, the RBI was buying dollars to pare the rupee's trade-weighted overvaluation after the dollar fell to a six-year low of INR43.12 following the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  revaluation.  \par \par \uc2 At 0820 GMT, the dollar was quoted at INR43.95.  \par \par \uc2 Despite the RBI dollar sales, analysts estimate that the rupee remains overvalued by more than 7% in trade-weighted terms. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will probably tolerate this for some time in order to fight a more serious threat - inflation.  \par \par \uc2 Inflation has remained the main policy challenge for the RBI since mid-2004. The bank has raised its key policy rate twice since then to stabilize prices and the latest spurt in global crude prices has aggravated this inflation threat.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Inflation To Rise Again \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To be sure, year-on-year inflation in India has eased in recent months but this is only because of a high-statistical-base effect, which is set to wear off by the end of this month.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After that, inflation should start rising, gaining additional upward momentum from the government's move Tuesday to raise local fuel prices to align them with high global crude prices.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The proposed INR3-a-liter increase in gasoline prices and INR2-a-liter hike in diesel prices could add 30 basis points to domestic inflation by late September, says Abheek Barua, economist at ABN Amro Bank India.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Inflation measured 3.08% in the week ended Aug. 20 from a year ago, down from 5.70% in early April.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  watchers believe the RBI will likely rely more on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  management than raising interest rates to counter the price increases.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The RBI may not prefer to raise interest rates much to fight inflation because such a move would expose Indian banks, which hold large amounts of government bonds, to high interest-rate risk," said an economist at a U.K.-based bank.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  isn't expected to raise its reverse repurchase rate, currently at 5%, more than a modest 25 basis points during the rest of 2005. The bank last raised the rate by 25 basis points in April.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Higher-than-expected export growth also will encourage the RBI to allow the rupee to remain overvalued in the near term. Exports rose 21% during the April-to-July period from a year earlier, well above 16% growth forecast by the government for the current fiscal year, which began April 1.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The RBI is also unlikely to worry that a strong rupee, which makes imports cheaper, could help inflate the trade and current account deficits to unmanageable proportions.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In its annual report released last week, the RBI said India's trade deficit would "remain within acceptable limits that can be financed by normal capital flows."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The current level of the trade deficit appears to be manageable and...consistent with India's growth aspirations," it added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India recorded a current account deficit of $6.43 billion in fiscal 2004-2005, compared with a $10.56 billion surplus the previous year, as the trade deficit more than doubled to $38.13 billion from $15.45 billion.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some in the market expect \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to limit the rupee's movements to a small range for some time, since both a rise or fall of the rupee could harm the economy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Barring temporary sharp fluctuations, I expect the RBI to keep the rupee broadly in a INR43.40-INR43.80 range for some time," said Mohan Shenoi, head of treasury at Mumbai-based Kotak Mahindra Bank.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "A rupee fall beyond this can be inflationary, while a sharp rise could fuel a consumption-led widening of the current account deficit," he said. "The RBI wouldn't want to encourage either."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Anil Varma, Dow Jones Newswires, 91 22 22884212; anil.varma@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Anil Varma covers India's money and fixed income markets for Dow Jones Newswires)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Iain McDonald [ 06-09-05 0836GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050906e196000et\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
