\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee ends at 8-\u189\'00\'BD mo low, cbank supports  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Anirban Nag  \par \uc2 599 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 August 2005\par 04:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates to close)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Aug 30 (Reuters) - The rupee fell to its weakest close since mid-December on Tuesday, easing beyond 44 to the dollar on mounting concerns that costly crude oil would widen India's trade deficit.  \par \par \uc2 The market was also weighed down by cash dollar shortage to fund immediate import needs, the dollar's strength overseas, arbitrage between offshore and onshore forward markets and worries about slowing foreign investment, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 "Oil companies have been on a dollar buying spree in the past few sessions," said N.S. Paramsivam, head of treasury at Essar group, Mumbai. "The rupee is catching up with fundamentals like a widening trade deficit, the dollar's overseas strength and weaker Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."  \par \par \uc2 The rupee, which analysts say is overvalued on a trade-weighted basis, ended at 44.0325/0375 per dollar, its lowest since Dec. 14 and down from the previous day's 43.885/890.  \par \par \uc2 It fell as low as 44.15, stretching losses to 1 percent in two sessions, before suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped pull it back, dealers said. \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  usually steps in to curb excessive volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2 JPMorgan said in a report on Monday, it expected the rupee to weaken to 45 by end-December.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not very strong as it would drive up the spot rupee and increase the price differential between the onshore and offshore forward markets.  \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was sporadic," said the chief dealer at a private bank. "If they sell dollars aggressively, the arbitrage between the offshore and the onshore market will widen."  \par \par \uc2 One-month non-deliverable rupee forwards (NDFs) <PNDF> were quoted at 44.23/33 while on the onshore market, the one-month forward rupee was at 44.05/06, prompting foreign investors to buy dollars in the local market and sell them overseas.  \par \par \uc2 NDFs are offshore financial instruments used by foreign investors and companies to bet on the value of the partially convertible rupee.  \par \par \uc2 OIL A BIG WORRY  \par \par \uc2 Crude oil futures, which hit a record $70.80 a barrel on Monday ahead of Hurricane Katrina's impact, were trading around $68 a barrel. Oil is India's top import and its price has risen more than 60 percent this year.  \par \par \uc2 India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in its annual report released on Monday that the merchandise trade deficit was expected to widen in 2005/06 due to substantially higher oil and non-oil imports and would push up the current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll conducted last month showed India's trade deficit was forecast to widen to a record $47 billion for 2005/06, from $38.13 billion a year earlier. A widening deficit means imports are outpacing exports and demand for the dollar is expected to increase to pay for them.  \par \par \uc2 The rupee has so far been supported by strong capital flows into India's stock market. Net investment of $7.4 billion this year has cushioned some of the adverse impact of high oil prices. The rupee hit a six-year high after China revalued in July.  \par \par \uc2 But the BSE share index <.BSESN> has slid 2.2 percent since hitting an all-time high on Aug. 18, partly on fears that India would have to raise retail fuel prices. That move could raise costs for manufacturers and crimp consumer spending in Asia's third-largest economy.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE (UPDATE 3)|LANGEN|Table|SNS|SNI|ABN|M|RBN|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050830e18u0012d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
