\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW:  \highlight22\uc2 India \highlight\uc2  May Cut  \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention \highlight\uc2  - Barclays  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Anil Varma  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 657 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 August 2005\par 12:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 COCHIN, \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Dow Jones)--A looming inflation threat could spur the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  to suspend or scale back \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in coming months and let the rupee rise in a bid to temper price pressures, said Barclays Capital's Singapore-based economist, Dominique Dwor-Frecaut.  \par \par \uc2 Dwor-Frecaut expects high global oil prices will push up India's inflation rate in coming quarters, but said the RBI will likely rely more on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  management than interest rate policy to counter the price increases.  \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not prefer to raise interest rates much to fight inflation because such a move would expose Indian banks, which hold large amounts of government bonds, to high interest rate risk," Dwor-Frecaut told Dow Jones Newswires in a recent interview in the southern Indian city of Cochin.  \par \par \uc2 "That leaves the exchange rate as the main option for checking inflation - the RBI may allow some rupee appreciation if India's inflation accelerates as expected," she added.  \par \par \uc2 The economist forecasts the dollar will fall to INR43.00 by end-December from INR43.53 Wednesday afternoon. A stronger rupee would lower import costs and help moderate inflation.  \par \par \uc2 The RBI has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in recent weeks to prevent the rupee, which analysts say? is currently overvalued in trade terms by nearly 9%, from appreciating further.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dollar buying has pulled the rupee down from a six-year high of INR43.12, which was hit on July 22 after China revalued its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 RBI \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserve data show that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought at least $5 billion in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in the two weeks ended Aug. 5. The bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves measured $142.64 billion on Aug. 5, up $23.30 billion from a year earlier.  \par \par \uc2 However, the bank probably won't sustain such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the coming months since the latest spurt in global crude prices has aggravated the threat of inflation in India, said Dwor-Frecaut.  \par \par \uc2 India's year-on-year inflation rate has eased in recent weeks due to a high-statistical-base effect. The rate fell to 3.84% toward end-July from 5.70% in early April. But this is likely a temporary trend.  \par \par \uc2 Dwor-Frecaut expects the government to raise local oil prices in the near term, which would fuel inflation, to realign them with global crude oil prices, which recently crossed the $65-per-barrel mark.  \par \par \uc2 Apart from scaled-back \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , continued capital inflows also could support the rupee during the rest of the year, she said.  \par \par \uc2 "Equity-related inflows to India should continue because of the economy's strong prospects, unless there is a broad sell-off across emerging markets," said Dwor-Frecaut. "Indian equity isn't yet considered expensive."  \par \par \uc2 Foreigners have bought Indian stocks worth $7.3 billion so far in 2005, compared with a record-high $9 billion in the whole of 2004.  \par \par \uc2 Dwor-Frecaut's expectation of possible rupee gains in the coming months differs from the view of some other analysts, who expect the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be pressured lower by a deepening current account deficit. India recorded a current account deficit of 6.43 billion deficit in the fiscal year ended March 31, compared with a $10.56 billion surplus in the previous year.  \par \par \uc2 But Dwor-Frecaut believes India's current account shortfall will be more than offset by its capital account surplus in the current fiscal year.  \par \par \uc2 "India will have a balance of payments surplus this year," she said.  \par \par \uc2 In 2004-2005, the country's overall balance of payments registered a $26.16 billion surplus despite the current account gap, mainly due to a surge in capital inflows.  \par \par \uc2 -By Anil Varma, Dow Jones Newswires; 91 22 22884212; anil.varma@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Iain McDonald [ 17-08-05 1040GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050817e18h000ge\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
