\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-INTERVIEW-India may allow rupee rise-Barclays Cap official  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Anirban Nag  \par \uc2 581 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 August 2005\par 05:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Repeats story issued on Sunday)  \par \par \uc2 COCHIN, India, Aug 14 (Reuters) - India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to loosen its tight grip over the rupee and allow it to appreciate from current levels to curb inflationary pressures, a senior official at Barclays Capital said on Sunday.  \par \par \uc2 "Oil prices are at $65 a barrel and the government will have to increase retail prices," Dominique Dwor-Frecaut, a director at Barclays Capital, Singapore told Reuters in an interview on the sidelines of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Assembly of India.  \par \par \uc2 "Also, demand pressures are not yet showing up in the inflation index now, but they are building up."  \par \par \uc2 The Indian government is not willing to take fiscal measures such as cutting taxes on fuel products, while there is also some political pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to go slow on interest rate increases, Dwor-Frecaut said.  \par \par \uc2 "So, by default, the exchange rate is the best tool to control inflation. I think the rupee will rise to 43 against the dollar by December's end," she said.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb excessive volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2 It has bought an estimated $5 billion in the two weeks to Aug. 5, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the partially convertible rupee <INR=IN> rose to a six-year high of 43.12 per dollar on July 22, a day after China revalued the yuan <CNY=CFXS> by 2.1 percent.  \par \par \uc2 That \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  swelled reserves to $142.6 billion and the rupee has since fallen by nearly a percent to 43.54 per dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The rupee has held its ground since January despite the dollar's rise overseas, supported by foreign fund investments of $7.2 billion this year into India's booming stock market.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts say the rupee is overvalued by nearly 9 percent on a trade-weighted basis and expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to continue its dollar buying to keep exports competitive.  \par \par \uc2 "A higher rupee will translate into cheaper imports, bringing down inflation," Dwor-Frecaut said.  \par \par \uc2 STRONG DEMAND  \par \par \uc2 Dwor-Frecaut said there was strong demand for goods and services in Asia's third largest economy, which is on course for 7 percent growth for the year to March. India's economy has expanded at an average of 7.7 percent in the past two years.  \par \par \uc2 Bank credit has grown at more than 30 percent and industrial production expanded by 11.7 percent in the year to June -- the fastest in nearly a decade -- as companies produced more to feed growing demand from Indian consumers.  \par \par \uc2 "Under such circumstances, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should raise interest rates. But they haven't done it in July and this has left me very confused," she said.  \par \par \uc2 "It will be more difficult to tighten monetary policy later on when there is a surge in inflation."  \par \par \uc2 Last month, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  held the key short term interest rate steady at 5 percent at its quarterly review, saying it wanted to ensure India's growth momentum despite inflation worries caused by record prices of oil, India's biggest import.  \par \par \uc2 It will review policy again in October, when most analysts expect it will raise the interest rate by 25 basis points.  \par \par \uc2 Inflation has dipped to 3.84 percent, but economists expect it to rise to 5 percent by end-December as demand pressures show up and high oil prices kick in.  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-INDIA-RUPEE (INTERVIEW, REPEAT)|LANGEN|SNS|SNI  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050816e18g0004r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
