\par\par \b \uc2 India Markets-Shares in biggest fall for 3 mths, rupee dips  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Annapurni Hariharan  \par \uc2 468 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 August 2005\par 15:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 BOMBAY, Aug 8 (Reuters) - Indian shares tumbled nearly 2 percent on Monday after running up more than a quarter since early May as investors worried about the high share valuations, while a cash dollar shortage weighed on the rupee.  \par \par \uc2 The BSE index <.BSESN> ended 1.91 percent lower at 7,606.17 points, the biggest one-day percentage fall since April 29 when the index dipped 2.05 percent.  \par \par \uc2 "The market was bound to correct at this level and it's a healthy one. But if liquidity tightens, then market could see more declines," said R. Sreesankar, research head at IL&FS Investsmart Ltd.  \par \par \uc2 Indian shares had galloped nearly 26 percent higher since the beginning of May, riding largely on robust foreign fund inflows and a wave of optimism as companies posted sturdy profits.  \par \par \uc2 Foreign funds have pumped in nearly $7 billion in 2005, compared with $8.5 billion for the whole of last year.  \par \par \uc2 But there were some concerns about a threat to corporate earnings from higher global crude oil prices, which hit a record high of nearly $63 a barrel on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Reliance Industries Ltd. slid nearly 2 percent to 711.50 rupees after having climbed more than 32 percent since the middle of June, when the two brothers of the founding Ambani family reached a settlement on an ownership dispute.  \par \par \uc2 The only stocks among the BSE index shares to have ended higher were state-run power producer National Thermal Power Corp. and oil explorer Oil and Natural Gas Corp. .  \par \par \uc2 The rupee slid, dragged down by a cash dollar shortage that traders said had been triggered by the Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sustained mop-up of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent sessions.  \par \par \uc2 The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  resumed its rupee-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month, after a three-month gap, to pull the rupee back from a six-year peak of 43.115/135 struck in the wake of China's yuan revaluation.  \par \par \uc2 The rupee <INR=IN> closed at 43.5475/5525 per dollar, 0.15 percent weaker than its previous close of 43.4825/4850.  \par \par \uc2 Federal bonds eased as traders lightened positions amid concerns about inflation with oil prices near all-time peaks.  \par \par \uc2 Traders were cautious ahead of Tuesday's meeting of the U.S. Federal Open Market Committee, which is widely expected to raise interest rates by a quarter point to 3.5 percent, but a surge in funds in India's banking system limited the fall in bonds.  \par \par \uc2 The yield on the popular 7.27 percent 2013 bond <IN072713G=> ended at 6.8811 percent, above Saturday's 6.8795 percent.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-WRAP|LANGEN|SNS|SNI|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050808e18800195\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
