\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Firmer on China remark but caution prevails  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Dhara Ranasinghe  \par \uc2 794 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Juli 2005\par 05:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, July 28 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  crept higher against the dollar on Thursday, recovering some of this week's losses after a Chinese newspaper report that China would allow markets to decide yuan rates.  \par \par \uc2 But gains were limited and traders said uncertainty remained over just how much China would allow its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to appreciate following the yuan's 2.1 percent revaluation one week ago.  \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar <SGD=> firmed 0.4 percent to 1.6633 and the Thai baht <THB=> added almost a quarter of a percent to 41.67 per dollar -- above Wednesday's one-week low at about 41.80.  \par \par \uc2 The South Korean won <KRW=KFTC><KRW=> gained almost half a percent to 1,026.3 per dollar, with hefty foreign buying of local equities also boosting the won.  \par \par \uc2 The official Shanghai Securities News quoted a senior central banker as saying China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would allow market forces to decide its yuan exchange rates following the yuan revaluation.  \par \par \uc2 "China is stating the logical path for a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  but we need to be very mindful that this is not going to happen today or tomorrow," said Philip Wee, senior market strategist at DBS Bank.  \par \par \uc2 "It is still a process, one that is likely to be slow. Markets generally have been divided over what China will do and trading has been listless."  \par \par \uc2 Comments from Chinese authorities in recent days have dampened talk that further rises in the yuan would follow the revaluation, undermining Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Markets remained sceptical about a rapid appreciation of the yuan.  \par \par \uc2 Yuan non-deliverable forward markets showed the derivatives priced in a mere 0.2 percent gain in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a month <CNYNDF=>, a sharp cut back from a 0.9 percent appreciation they had factored in on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 "On the one hand there is room for further disappointment with how little China has actually done, but on the other hand economic data is improving and growth expectations have improved," said UBS \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Ashley Davies.  \par \par \uc2 The yuan <CNY=CFXS> strengthened to a post-revaluation high of 8.1080 per dollar and closed at this level.  \par \par \uc2 TAX FOCUS FOR PESO  \par \par \uc2 The Philippine peso <PHP=> briefly tested Wednesday's two-week trough at about 56.20 per dollar before firming alongside other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 News late on Wednesday that the country's Supreme Court may not decide until mid-August whether to lift its freeze on an expanded sales tax initially pressured the peso. A ruling on the key tax change had been expected in the first week of August.  \par \par \uc2 Worries about the delay in implementing the tax, together with a political crisis, have dealt the peso a blow. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has shed about four percent against the dollar since early May.  \par \par \uc2 "We've got a delay on the tax ruling, but the yen is also stronger so this is helping the peso a bit," said a Manila trader.  \par \par \uc2 The Indonesian rupiah <IDR=> was the day's laggard, trading steady from late Asian trading levels on Wednesday at about 9,835 per dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "Demand for the dollar is strong and coming from government-owned companies importing oil. Also, there is some month-end demand from corporates," said a Jakarta trader.  \par \par \uc2 Indonesian demand for oil means extra demand for dollars, and that has resulted in rupiah sales. Dealers said that while the Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been checking rates in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market there had been no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to bolster the rupiah.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and debt markets in \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's financial capital Bombay were shut for a second day after heavy rains. \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Change on the day at   0803 GMT\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       112.65         112.66       +0.01\par  Sing dlr        1.6666         1.6700       +0.20\par  Taiwan dlr      31.949         31.970       +0.07\par  Korean won     1027.70        1031.10       +0.33\par  Baht             41.79          41.77       -0.05\par  Peso             56.04          56.18       +0.26\par  Rupiah         9835.00        9834.00       -0.01\par  Change on year\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       112.65         102.45       -9.05\par  Sing dlr        1.6666         1.6310       -2.14\par  Taiwan dlr      31.949         31.920       -0.09\par  Korean won     1027.70        1035.10       +0.72\par  Baht             41.79          38.88       -6.96\par  Peso             56.04          56.28       +0.44\par  Rupiah         9835.00        9290.00       -5.54 ((Editing by\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 John Mair, Reuters Messaging: dhara.ranasinghe.reuters.com@reuters.net, +65 6870 3840))  \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-ASIA-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|SI|Z