\par\par \b \uc2 = Indian Exporters Give Yuan Revaluation A Cautious Cheer  \b0\par\par\uc2 632 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juli 2005\par 22:05\par \uc2 OsterDowJones Commodity Wire\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2005, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  Standard Attribution Statement: News Provided by COMTEX.  \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI, Jul 22, 2005 (ODC via Comtex) --  \par \par \uc2 \f2 By P.R. Venkat\par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 India's exporters, who have long complained of China's unfair \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  advantage, say the yuan's revaluation will help level the playing field. But analysts say the real benefits to India will depend on the rupee's behavior in coming weeks.  \par \par \uc2 China has had an artificial peg for a long time, Commerce and Industry Minister Kamal Nath told reporters Friday, when asked to comment on the revaluation. "This should be corrected to some extent now," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Thursday, the People's Bank of China said the yuan's de facto peg to the dollar would be dropped in favor of a managed float against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In the process, the yuan was revalued about 2%, to CNY8.1100 to the dollar from CNY8.2765.  \par \par \uc2 Nath said India's textile exports in particular would benefit from China's move.  \par \par \uc2 Others said global demand for India's textile products and gems and jewelry would rise as China's exports become costlier.  \par \par \uc2 India's textile industry, which contributes about 23% to total exports of $80 billion, is struggling to compete with China after the elimination of quotas that regulated world trade until Jan. 1 this year.  \par \par \uc2 Gems and jewelry account for nearly 17% of India's exports.  \par \par \uc2 India's textile companies have said China's undervalued \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gives its products a price advantage in key markets such as the U.S. and E.U.  \par \par \uc2 The yuan revaluation "will benefit us slightly," said Siddhartha Rajagopal, executive director of the Cotton Textiles Export Promotion Council.  \par \par \uc2 Rajagopal said that while China had a 40% cost advantage in textiles in the past due to its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels, the advantage will now narrow by 5%.  \par \par \uc2 "Even a small gain like this will help," said Rafeeque Ahmed, a leather exporter and former president of the Federation of Indian Exporters Organization, or FIEO.  \par \par \uc2 Ahmed said there are prospects for a further appreciation of the yuan and this will increase business for India from the U.S. and the E.U., key markets for both India and China.  \par \par \uc2 The key question for exporters now is how the rupee responds to global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  changes due to the revaluation.  \par \par \uc2 Ajay Sahai, director general of FIEO, said Indian exporters will be happy with a rupee level of INR43.50 to the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "Any level below INR43.50 will be cause for worry," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Soon after China's move, the rupee strengthened, hitting a six-year high of INR43.14 against the dollar Friday.  \par \par \uc2 It ended at INR43.47 to the dollar after suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier in the day to pull the rupee down.  \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  probably also wants to keep the rupee's overvaluation in check," said a dealer at a local bank.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts say the rupee is currently overvalued by more than 10% against a basket of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in trade-weighted terms.  \par \par \uc2 Stock-related capital inflows and broad dollar weakness have pushed the rupee up 6.2% against the dollar over the past 12 months, hurting exporters.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  watchers say the rupee is likely to hold its ground or even strengthen modestly in the near term as it adjusts fully to the yuan revaluation.  \par \par \uc2 The commerce minister Friday said India will retain its export target of $92 billion for the current financial year that ends on March 31, 2006, a 15% rise on the year.  \par \par \uc2 "We will stick to it despite the yuan's revaluation," said Nath.  \par \par \uc2 -By P.R. Venkat, Dow Jones Newswires; +91-11-2307 4020; venkat.pr@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 (Anil Varma also contributed to this article)  \par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires  \par \par \uc2 07-22-05 0931ET  \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020050722e17m002hr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
