\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-Indian construction firm L&T bullish on rupee  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Annapurni Hariharan and Suresh Seshadri  \par \uc2 594 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Juli 2005\par 10:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 BOMBAY, July 7 (Reuters) - Larsen & Toubro Ltd. , a major Indian engineering and construction services exporter, sees the rupee gaining 2.6 percent in the next nine months as foreign investment into the country swells, a senior official said.  \par \par \uc2 L&T generated a third of its $3.3 billion revenue in the last financial year from exports and is a key player in India's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2 R. Govindan, its treasurer, told Reuters on Thursday he sees the rupee strengthening to 42.50 per dollar <INR=IN> by March 2006, which would be an increase of 2.6 percent compared with 43.61 on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 He said he expects foreign investment in infrastructure projects in Asia's fourth-biggest economy to boost the rupee, while oil-and-gas finds in India would offer some cushion for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against soaring international crude costs.  \par \par \uc2 "It used to be a small market, international flows used to be small... you used to have capital shortage. Now, the service sector has become a very serious component of trade or the flow of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said.  \par \par \uc2 India's economy is forecast to grow 7 percent in the financial year ending March 2006 after expanding 6.9 percent last year.  \par \par \uc2 A 9.3 percent rise in services output, which now accounts for half of gross domestic product, powered growth in the year to March 2005.  \par \par \uc2 External trade represents about a third of India's GDP with invisible receipts and payments -- mainly comprising services sector flows -- up 46 percent and 70 percent on the year respectively in 2004/05, to $31.7 billion and $45.8 billion.  \par \par \uc2 L&T has interests in the Middle East, Africa and Central Asia.  \par \par \uc2 SEES \b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK INTERVENING\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Govindan said he expected the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) to continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  by buying dollars in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to curb rupee strength.  \par \par \uc2 "It makes sense for the RBI over the next two to three years to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at lower (dollar) levels to provide a cushioning impact for the external capital that is coming in, as stable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  conditions are preferred by long-term investors."  \par \par \uc2 Limiting the rupee's gains also helps Indian exporters stay competitive on price.  \par \par \uc2 "They should keep accumulating dollars at lower levels so that it encourages foreigners to pour in money and we can use the money to create infrastructure," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Overhauling and modernising India's rickety infrastructure would also be a big draw for foreign investors, which would boost the rupee.  \par \par \uc2 "Probably we are one of the few countries where the infrastructure is really creaking," he said.  \par \par \uc2 "Our assessment is over three to four years the civil infrastructure, roads, ports, dams, buildings, water and process industries will see at least $275 billion of investments."  \par \par \uc2 Foreign funds -- including first-time investors from Japan and South Korea -- have pumped more than $1.5 billion into Indian shares since June 1, attracted by India's growth prospects.  \par \par \uc2 Govindan said domestic oil-and-gas finds plus overseas acquisitions by the country's top explorer, Oil and Natural Gas Corp. Ltd. , would help offset the impact on the rupee of a record-breaking rally in international crude prices.  \par \par \uc2 India imports about 70 percent of its crude, making the country's widening trade gap, and therefore the rupee, vulnerable to sharp spikes in international crude prices. ($1=43.58 Indian rupees)  \par \par \uc2 FINANCIAL-INDIA-L&T (INTERVIEW)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|SNS|SNI  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050707e177000oi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
