\par\par \b \uc2 RBI WATCH: India Lets Rupee Rise, Helps Fight Inflation  \b0\par\par\uc2 948 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Juni 2005\par 07:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Anil Varma \par    A Dow Jones Newswires Column \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee is more expensive now than at anytime in more than eight years against a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from India's biggest trading partners, but unusually the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  seems little worried.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Rather than \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to stem the rupee's rise and keep India's exports competitive as it might have done in the past, the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  has scaled back its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Analysts say the RBI is letting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciate to help it fight rising inflation and is unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  strongly in the near future.  \par \par \uc2 The rupee is regarded at fair value when an index of its real effective exchange rate, which both the RBI and private-sector economists calculate, is around 100.  \par \par \uc2 But analysts say the index, which measures the rupee against five \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is now 107.10, indicating that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is overvalued by around 7%. That is the largest overvaluation since the Asian financial crisis in 1997.  \par \par \uc2 The RBI last issued an official estimate of the exchange rate index as on April 15, when the index was 107.41.  \par \par \uc2 This change in tactic by the RBI in allowing an overvalued exchange rate indicates that, to the extent possible, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  prefers such indirect methods to counter inflation than a more aggressive approach of increasing interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been pushed higher in the past 10 months by heavy portfolio investment inflows attracted to \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strong economy. The RBI \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to clip the rupee's gains during February and March, but market participants say there has been a significant decline in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dollar buying since the second half of April.  \par \par \uc2 The decrease in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has helped push down \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's previously fast-growing \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to $139.65 billion on May 20, from a record-high $142.55 billion a month earlier.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par      Strong Rupee Keeps Lid On Inflation \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The rupee's sustained strength in recent months has dented a favorite theory of many market watchers that the RBI doesn't want the rupee to stray more than 5% from its fair value level.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At 0400 GMT Friday, the dollar was at INR.43.64, above a near-six-year low of INR43.26 set on May 12. The rupee has gained 6.5% against the dollar in the past 10 months and has also rallied against the euro in recent months, benefiting from the single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's general weakness. The euro was last quoted INR53.61, down 10.5% from an all-time high of INR59.88 in November 2004.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The RBI hasn't strongly resisted the rupee's overvaluation in recent months, probably because \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strength helps moderate inflation," said Amit Bansal, head of treasury at Barclays Bank India. "If you look back, the key policy moves made by the RBI in the past one year or so were aimed at containing inflation."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 High inflation fueled by surging global oil prices has been the biggest challenge to the RBI's monetary policy in the past year, forcing the bank to tighten policy twice since October 2004 and reverse a four-year easing trend.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The RBI forecasts inflation of 5.0%-5.5% for the fiscal year that started April 1 and has said that price stability is one of its prime policy objectives. In its annual policy paper issued late April, the bank warned that the impact of high global oil prices is yet to be fully transmitted to the domestic economy and as a result "upside risks" to inflation persist.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India's inflation hit a three-and-a-half-year high of 8.74% in August 2004, but has moderated since then and was 5.55% in the week ended May 14.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Aside from statistical reasons and a government decision not to pass on to domestic oil product prices increases in global prices, the rupee's rise has helped reduce inflation.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par      Rising Rupee OK While Exports Rise \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Market watchers believe economics will eventually prevail over politics and the government will raise local oil prices sooner or later so the RBI will likely continue to tolerate an overvalued rupee, particularly as the country's exports are showing no sign of weakness.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Exports grew 24.4% in the fiscal year ended March 31, well above the official target of 16.0%. The government has maintained a similar target for the current fiscal year. Exports grew 17.2% in April.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Aside from helping keep a lid on inflation, an appreciating \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will encourage capital inflows,  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The RBI is probably not in a hurry to see the rupee depreciate because that would discourage foreign portfolio investors who are looking at India," said a treasury official at a local bank.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India received record-high portfolio inflows - over $9 billion - in 2004 and through the first quarter of 2005, as the country's rallying stock market and a strong rupee attracted global investors. Such inflows helped offset the country's current account deficit.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, portfolio investments have turned negative more recently. Foreigners pulled out $656 million from India's equity and debt markets during April and May, after investing $3.84 billion during the January-to-March quarter.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Given the recent weakness in portfolio inflows, the RBI may allow rupee overvaluation for some more time," added the bank official.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Anil Varma, Dow Jones Newswires, 91 22 22884212; anil.varma@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Anil Varma covers India's money and fixed-income markets for Dow Jones Newswires)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Iain McDonald [ 03-06-05 0504GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1078 4282  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050603e16300045\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
