\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-TECHNICALS-Indian rupee seen weakening in medium term  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Suresh Seshadri  \par \uc2 512 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Juni 2005\par 05:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Repeats story from late on Tuesday)  \par \par \uc2 BOMBAY, May 31 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee is set to weaken further in coming weeks, after slipping off a 5-1/2-year peak in mid-May, with most technical indicators signalling more losses against the buoyant dollar, technical analysts said on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 The rupee has fallen 1.1 percent to a one-month low since hitting the 5-1/2-year intra-day high of 43.26 per dollar on May 11.  \par \par \uc2 The slide has accelerated in the past two sessions with the market nervous that foreign funds are cutting their exposure to Indian assets. It was at 43.735 per dollar <INR=IN> at 1100 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 "Most indicators point clearly to a positive divergence since the rupee hit 43.26, which signals that the dollar should gain against the rupee in the medium term," said T. Gnanasekar, a chartist and director at Commtrendz Research.  \par \par \uc2 "Charts point to a near-term support for rupee at 43.85, with a break pointing to 44.50 which is the 38.2 (percent) retracement of the rupee's move from 46.49 in July 2004 to 43.26."  \par \par \uc2 The rupee, which is only partially convertible on the capital account, has lost 0.5 percent so far in 2005, after gaining 10.6 percent in the three years from December 31, 2001.  \par \par \uc2 Worries about a widening trade deficit, a slowdown in foreign investment in the local stock market and the dollar's rebound overseas have all weighed on the rupee, which had been supported in the past couple of years by robust portfolio fund inflows.  \par \par \uc2 NEAR-TERM LESS BEARISH  \par \par \uc2 Technical analysts warn that rupee charting can be distorted by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action as the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  frequently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth volatility.  \par \par \uc2 The outlook for the rupee is, however, less bearish from a near-term perspective, with charts showing it well supported at 43.85, a level not breached on a closing basis since January 10.  \par \par \uc2 "In the immediate term, 43.85 is still a very strong resistance level for the dollar, as it is a multiple top," said R.K. Gurumurthy, vice president on the proprietary trading desk at ING Financial Markets in Singapore.  \par \par \uc2 "On as many as six occasions, it has failed to break past that level."  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said a positive divergence on the moving averages convergence divergence (MACD) momentum indicator, created by the dollar not hitting a matching low on the indicator when the rupee scaled 43.26, also pointed to more weakness for the rupee.  \par \par \uc2 "An Elliot wave based on a retracement from the last move off 44.08 resistance for the dollar on January 10 should also weigh once the rupee weakens past 43.85," said K. Rajaram, head \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk management at textiles exporter Arvind Mills Ltd.  \par \par \uc2 The Elliot Wave principle applied to price charts shows wave patterns in a series of what are called impulse and corrective waves.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE (TECHNICALS)|LANGEN|SNS|SNI  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050601e1610004m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
