\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee at new 3-month high, near 5-year peak  \b0\par\par\uc2 401 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Mai 2005\par 05:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 BOMBAY, May 5 (Reuters) - The rupee rose to end at a new three-month high on Thursday, on continued speculation of a yuan revaluation and a rally in local stocks after India proposed scrapping a voting rights cap in private-sector banks.  \par \par \uc2 The rupee <INR=IN> ended at 43.4200/4250 per dollar, its strongest close since Feb. 3 and 0.2 percent firmer on the day. It inched up to 43.3850 per dollar in intra-day trade, within sight of a five-year peak of 43.30 hit in early February.  \par \par \uc2 "Everything in India is against the dollar. Stocks are rising, there are fresh reforms for the banking sector and the yuan revaluation talks still remain," said a dealer at a state-run bank.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said talk that China would eventually revalue the yuan refused to go away, even though China's finance minister said speculation made it hard for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reform right now.  \par \par \uc2 Speculation over a possible yuan revaluation has helped lift the rupee and other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the past two weeks.  \par \par \uc2 In overseas trade, the dollar remained weak against the euro and the yen as investors focussed on news about China and an uncertain outlook in the United States ahead of Friday's jobs data.  \par \par \uc2 The market was, however, wary of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively earlier this year to check the rupee's gains.  \par \par \uc2 But some analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not be as worried about rupee strength as it was in February, as it was now focused on containing inflation pressures after raising the key short-term interest rate last week.  \par \par \uc2 A JP Morgan real effective exchange rate index <JPMREER> shows the rupee is overvalued by nearly 5 percent.  \par \par \uc2 India's government approved a proposal late on Wednesday to scrap a 10 percent voting rights cap in private sector banks, a move which analysts said would attract more foreign investment into local banks.  \par \par \uc2 The proposal now has to be approved by parliament, and communist parties which support the coalition government say they will vote against the plan. For more, please double-click on [ID:nBOM14735].   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -------------------------------------------------------------\par                                MARKET SNAPSHOT\par  Bombay Sensitive Index          <.BSESN>\par  Indian rupee (/$)               <INR=IN>\par  7.38 percent 2015 bond (%)      <IN073815G=>\par -------------------------------------------------------------\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|RBN|SNS|SNI|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050505e155000qk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
