\par\par \b \uc2 India rupee at 2-wk high on inflows, weaker dollar.  \b0\par\par\uc2 364 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 April 2005\par 14:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BOMBAY, April 7 (Reuters) - The rupee firmed 0.07 percent\par against the dollar on Thursday as dealers cited talk of foreign\par capital inflows and as the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  slipped against major\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     The rupee ended at 43.7250/7300 per dollar, its highest\par level since March 22, but dealers said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pair would\par likely drift, taking its cue from world oil prices and the\par dollar's performance against major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     "We have seen good amounts of inflows today. Also we\par haven't seen the RBI (Reserve Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as we\par had a couple of weeks ago," said one dealer. "It's only a small\par range today."\par     The dollar gave up recent gains, slipping to a one-week low\par against the euro on Thursday. It had pushed to two-month highs\par against the euro earlier this week on expectations the U.S.\par Federal Reserve would tighten monetary policy aggressively.\par     U.S.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  crude oil        was around $56.50 a barrel, off\par recent all-time highs above $58, but fears lingered that high\par prices of India's biggest import would widen the country's\par trade deficit, already at a record high.\par     India's trade deficit jumped to a record $28.3 billion in\par the first nine months of the past financial year that ended\par March 31.\par     Dealers said the Bank of England's widely expected decision\par to leave interest rates on hold would have little impact on the\par local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     Traders will be eyeing foreign fund investments in local\par shares, which at about $4 billion so far in 2005 are already\par nearly half the record $8.5 billion they bought in 2004.\par     Their purchases along with foreign direct investments,\par overseas borrowings by local firms and export remittances have\par supported the rupee. It set a five-year peak of 43.30 in early\par February, but retreated on aggressive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     The dollar-buying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pulled\par the rupee down 0.11 percent in fiscal 2004/05 (April-March),\par after an 8.7 percent gain in the previous year.\par  \par -------------------------------------------------------------\par                                   MARKET SNAPSHOT\par     Bombay Sensitive Index\par     Indian rupee (/$)\par     7.38 percent 2015 bond (%)\par -------------------------------------------------------------.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050407e147002gx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
