\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee, bonds slip, bank strike hits trade.  \b0\par\par\uc2 494 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2005\par 12:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BOMBAY, March 22 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee and\par government bonds slipped on Tuesday in thin trade as state-run\par banks, key players in both markets, were shut by a one-day\par nationwide strike by bank employees protesting against banking\par sector reforms.\par  Some one million bank workers went on strike to protest\par against government plans to encourage bank mergers, shut\par unviable branches and ease foreign direct investment rules,\par measures the workers fear will result in job losses.\par     Dealers in the $9 billion-a-day \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market\par said trading volumes were about half the daily average, with\par foreign banks and private banks fairly active. Some stray deals\par were also done by state-run banks, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  broker said.\par     The rupee          ended at a two-week low of 43.7200/7300\par for the second straight day, a tad weaker than Monday's close\par at 43.7225/7275 as the dollar clung to its broad gains ahead of\par an expected quarter percentage point increase in U.S. rates.\par     The U.S.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Federal Reserve is widely expected to raise\par interest rates later on Tuesday, but dealers are waiting to see\par the Fed's accompanying statement, since the dollar has gained\par on speculation it would signal a quicker pace of future rate\par rises.\par     "The market wanted to square positions before the FOMC\par (Federal Open Market Committee)," said a senior dealer at a\par foreign bank. But he said the rupee might find some support\par from continuing strong inflows of foreign capital.\par     "There should be a good amount of capital inflows in the\par next few days so it would depend on the RBI (Reserve Bank of\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 )."\par     The Indian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  heavily in recent\par weeks to curb the rupee's gains in an effort to keep exports\par competitive and dealers will remain on watch for further\par activity. A report showed the RBI did not \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in January.\par     The Reserve Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which holds its annual policy\par meeting next month, mostly considers local factors while\par determining its monetary stance, but also takes global interest\par rate trends into account.\par     State-run banks are the largest investors in government\par bonds, with average daily turnover of about 30-35 billion\par rupees and dealers said Tuesday's trade was a paltry 7 billion\par rupees.\par     The yield on the benchmark 10-year bond              rose\par to 6.7149 percent, from the previous day's close at 6.7064\par percent as fears linger that high oil prices would rekindle\par inflationary pressures in Asia's fourth-biggest economy.\par     Corporate bond yields edged up with sentiment cautious\par ahead of the U.S. Federal Reserve's policy meeting later in the\par day.\par     The actively traded Power Finance Corp's 7.0 percent 2009\par bond was dealt at 7.11 percent, above the previous day's\par 7.08/10 percent. Deals were mainly in short-term and floating\par rate bonds.\par  \par -------------------------------------------------------------\par                                   MARKET SNAPSHOT\par     Bombay Sensitive Index\par     Indian rupee (/$)\par     7.38 percent 2015 bond (%)\par -------------------------------------------------------------.\par   \f28 \par \par 