\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee slips as high oil prices weigh.  \b0\par\par\uc2 332 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2005\par 05:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BOMBAY, March 18 (Reuters) - The rupee slipped against the\par dollar early on Friday, extending losses for the fourth day,\par with sentiment edgy on rising crude oil prices which analysts\par say could expand India's already widening trade deficit.\par     The rupee was also weighed down by a fall in the domestic\par stock market and a firmer dollar, which hung on to gains in\par Asian trade as investors pondered whether a Federal Reserve\par meeting on Tuesday would signal a faster pace of interest rate\par rises.\par     At 9:35 a.m (0355 GMT), the rupee          was quoted at\par 43.6800/6900 per dollar, weaker than Thursday's 43.6550/6650\par and further away from a five-week closing high of 43.55/56 on\par Monday.\par     "There are concerns about high oil prices and slowing\par foreign fund inflows into local stocks," said a dealer at a\par state-run bank.\par     U.S. crude futures hit a record $57.60 a barrel on\par Thursday, but eased in Asian trade to $56.15.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  In London, May\par Brent crude settled at $55.06 a barrel after hitting an\par all-time high of $56.15.\par     As crude oil is India's biggest import item, higher prices\par could increase India's trade deficit, which is on course for a\par record in the current financial year ending on March 31,\par analysts said.\par     The deficit during April to February widened to $23.83\par billion from $13.72 billion in the same period last year, as\par the expanding economy sucked in more imports.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  aggressively in\par the past few weeks by absorbing foreign fund inflows to keep\par exports competitive. The dollar purchases have boosted \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves to a record $137.56 billion.\par     Overseas portfolio investors, who have invested nearly $4\par billion in stocks this year, sold a total of about $56 million\par on Tuesday and Wednesday this week.\par  \par -------------------------------------------------------------\par                                   MARKET SNAPSHOT\par     Bombay Sensitive Index\par     Indian rupee (/$)\par     7.38 percent 2015 bond (%)\par -------------------------------------------------------------.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050318e13i001ur\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
