\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee seen dipping for 4th day, oil weighs.  \b0\par\par\uc2 306 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2005\par 03:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BOMBAY, March 18 (Reuters) - The rupee is expected to\par extend losses into a fourth day on Friday, as a watchful\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is worried about record high oil prices widening\par India's trade deficit, traders said.\par     The unit will also be weighed down by a firm dollar, which\par hung on to gains in Asian trade as investors pondered whether a\par Federal Reserve meeting on Tuesday would signal a faster rate\par rise.\par     U.S. crude futures hit a record $57.60 a barrel on\par Thursday, but eased in Asian trade to $56.15. In London, May\par Brent crude settled at $55.06 a barrel, after hitting an\par all-time high of $56.15.\par     As crude oil is India's biggest import item the firm prices\par could increase India's trade deficit, which is on course for a\par record in the current financial year ending on March 31,\par analysts said.\par     The deficit during April to February widened to $23.83\par billion from $13.72 billion in the same period last year, as\par the expanding economy sucked in more imports.\par   \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  aggressively in\par the past few weeks by absorbing foreign fund inflows to keep\par exports competitive. The dollar purchases have boosted \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves to a record $137.56 billion.\par     Foreign funds have pumped in nearly $4 billion so far this\par year, almost half of the record $8.5 billion in all of 2004, on\par expectations that economic growth of at least 7 percent this\par year will lift corporate earnings.\par     The rupee          ended Thursday at 43.6550/6650, down\par from the previous close of 43.63/64 and further away from a\par five-week closing high of 43.55/56 on Monday.\par  \par -------------------------------------------------------------\par                                   MARKET SNAPSHOT\par     Bombay Sensitive Index\par     Indian rupee (/$)\par     7.38 percent 2015 bond (%)\par -------------------------------------------------------------.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050318e13i000uv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
