\par\par \b \uc2 Indian cbank pushes down rupee for third day.  \b0\par\par\uc2 349 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2005\par 13:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BOMBAY, March 17 (Reuters) - The rupee weakened for the\par third straight day on Thursday, as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stepped up\par its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on worries record high oil prices would worsen\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's widening trade deficit.\par     U.S. light crude gained 58 cents to $57.04 a barrel while\par London's Brent crude, benchmark for European imports, added 58\par cents to $55.46 a barrel.\par     The surge was triggered by a big draw in U.S. gasoline and\par heating oil inventories, forcing OPEC to consider a second\par output increase just a day after its deal to raise supplies\par failed to halt crude's record-breaking advance.\par     The rupee          ended at 43.6550/6650, down from the\par previous close of 43.63/64 and further away from a five-week\par closing high of 43.55/56 on Monday.\par     "The state-run banks came in towards close and pushed down\par the rupee to 43.66 from 43.63," said a trader at a private\par sector bank.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  "They seem pretty determined to keep a cap on the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's gains."\par     Traders said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was concerned that firm oil\par prices could increase India's trade deficit, which was on\par course for a record in the current financial year ending on\par March 31.\par     The trade deficit during April to February widened to\par $23.83 billion from $13.72 billion in the same period last\par year, as the expanding economy sucked in more imports.\par     But analysts say a part of the deficit is likely to be\par offset by rising foreign fund inflows into Asia's\par fourth-largest economy.\par     Foreign funds have pumped in nearly $4 billion so far this\par year, almost half of the record $8.5 billion in all of 2004 on\par expectations that economic growth of at least 7 percent this\par year will lift corporate earnings.\par    India's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has absorbed bulk of the inflows\par boosting \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves to a record $137.56 billion\par -- the sixth largest in the world.\par  \par -------------------------------------------------------------\par                                   MARKET SNAPSHOT\par     Bombay Sensitive Index\par     Indian rupee (/$)\par     7.38 percent 2015 bond (%)\par -------------------------------------------------------------.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050317e13h009qh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
