\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee eases for 2nd day, could dip more.  \b0\par\par\uc2 406 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2005\par 12:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BOMBAY, March 16 (Reuters) - The rupee lost ground for the\par second straight session on Wednesday, pulling away from recent\par five-week peaks, as commercial demand for the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par perked up amid a widening trade deficit, dealers said.\par     The local unit          ended at 43.63/64 per dollar, down\par from the previous close of 43.5850/5950 and off a five-week\par closing high of 43.55/56 on Monday.\par     "There was dollar demand from state-run and foreign banks\par which weighed on the rupee," said a chief dealer at a private\par sector bank. "The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will be comfortable with the\par move as this is what they have been wanting -- a weaker rupee."\par     Traders said a few banks had gone long on the dollar on\par expectations that commercial demand seen on Wednesday would\par spillover into the next session.\par     The rupee started on a weaker note after the dollar hit a\par one-week high against the euro on rising foreign investments in\par the world's largest economy.\par     But the U.S.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  unit lost some ground in European trade ahead\par of data which is expected to show the U.S. current account\par deficit widened to a record level in the fourth quarter.\par     The dollar's recent weakness should boost sentiment for the\par rupee but \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to curb\par gains in an attempt to keep exports cheap.\par     It has been \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  through state-run banks, who have\par been absorbing robust foreign equity flows into Asia's\par fourth-largest economy, dealers say.\par     Foreign funds have pumped in nearly $4 billion so far this\par year, almost half of the record $8.5 billion in all of 2004 on\par expectations that economic growth of at least 7 percent this\par year will lift corporate earnings.\par     The persistent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has boosted\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves to a record $137.56 billion -- the\par sixth largest in the world.\par     Analysts say foreign fund investments are likely to help in\par partly offsetting a record trade deficit expected for the\par financial year to March.\par     India's trade deficit for the April-February period widened\par to $23.83 billion from $13.72 billion in the same period last\par year, as the expanding economy sucked in more imports, and\par traders are concerned firm oil prices could increase the\par deficit.\par  \par -------------------------------------------------------------\par                                   MARKET SNAPSHOT\par     Bombay Sensitive Index\par     Indian rupee (/$)\par     7.38 percent 2015 bond (%)\par -------------------------------------------------------------.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050316e13g0059m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
