\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Asia raises its game to curb  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  strength.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Rajat Bhattacharya   \par \uc2 776 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2005\par 08:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, March 16 (Reuters) - Asian authorities, facing a fourth straight year of strengthening \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , are stepping up verbal warnings and appear to be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  more to shoo off speculators and keep their exporters competitive.    \par \par \uc2  The latest broadside has come from Taiwan.    \par \par \uc2  On Monday its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it was worried about a buildup of almost T$150 billion (US$4.8 billion) in uninvested Taiwan dollar holdings by foreigners. It wants foreign investors to buy stocks or take the money home, aiming to discourage speculators from causing sharp fluctuations in the exchange rate.    \par \par \uc2  Central banks in South Korea, India and Thailand have also voiced concern about a surge in portfolio investments that has helped drive stock indices in some of those countries to record highs and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in some of them to their strongest levels since the 1997/98 financial crisis.    \par \par \uc2  A month ago Asian central banks seemed to be willing to let their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise against the dollar after a bout of heavy dollar-buying in 2004.    \par \par \uc2  But analysts said signs that China was still reluctant to allow its tightly pegged \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen had hardened the resolve of other countries to keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in check.    \par \par \uc2  "Nobody wants their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to gain too far compared with China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Mark Cranfield, head of treasury in Asia at Landesbank Baden-Wuerttemberg in Singapore.    \par \par \uc2  "When China does relax, they'll relax. Nobody wants to lose too much ground to China."    \par \par \uc2  Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserve figures showed signs of stepped up dollar-buying in February. Taiwan, South Korea, India, Singapore and Thailand together added $18 billion to their foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in February.    \par \par \uc2  That followed a $2.9 billion decline in those countries' total reserves in January. It compares with average monthly reverve gains of $11.1 billion during 2004 for the five.    \par \par \uc2  SALVO FROM SEOUL    \par \par \uc2  Last Thursday alone, traders said the Bank of Korea spent almost $2 billion to halt a surge in the won.    \par \par \uc2  "The idea that there may be more to come is credible," said David Simmonds, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Royal Bank of Scotland, referring to the South Korean dollar-buying. "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts may be quite persistent for a while."    \par \par \uc2  Japan, which has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since March 2004, has continued with its verbal threats to step in. The Bank of Japan is under relatively less pressure because the yen has underperformed other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far this year.    \par \par \uc2  But elsewhere, signs all point to \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies.    \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves surged about $8.7 billion in February alone, the largest increase among Asian central banks last month.    \par \par \uc2  Part of the rise came from more than $3 billion in foreign investments into the country's stocks this year, which drove the local benchmark stock index to a record high on March 9.    \par \par \uc2  RBI Governor Yaga Venugopal Reddy said last month that large foreign inflows posed a challenge to exchange rate management and monetary policy. The rupee has lost 0.2 percent this year.    \par \par \uc2  Thailand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been less verbal and more active in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets lately. The Bank of Thailand's net forward \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  contracts to sell the baht jumped to $7.7 billion as of March 4 from $4.6 billion at the end of 2004.    \par \par \uc2  Foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose by $1 billion in February.    \par \par \uc2  "The increase in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserve holdings, combined with the rise of forward commitments, underscores the policy desire to prevent a sharp appreciation in the baht and yet also tighten domestic liquidity," said Sin Beng Ong, an economist at JPMorgan Chase, in a report.    \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore (MAS) may also have less appetite for a gradually appreciating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The MAS uses the level of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as its main monetary policy tool.    \par \par \uc2  Emmanuel Ng, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN AMRO Bank in Singapore, expects the MAS to switch to a neutral bias for the Singapore dollar at its half-yearly monetary policy meeting next month after keeping an appreciating bias over the past year.    \par \par \uc2  Singapore reserves rose $1.9 billion in February. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has gained 0.5 percent this year, compared with 3 percent gains in the won and Taiwan dollar.    \par \par \uc2  Ng said Singapore had kept the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a "tight leash" this year because it was worried about a slowdown in electronics and biomedical industries.    \par \par \uc2  "Inflation is not perceived to be a major cause for concern and the pass-through effects of higher oil prices should be limited, weakening the argument that a stronger exchange rate would stem inflation," Ng said.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050316e13g002vl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
