\par\par \b \uc2 Indian Govt Bonds End Dn,Mkt Quiet Before New FY;Rupee Up  \b0\par\par\uc2 754 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2005\par 14:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--Indian government bond prices ended slightly lower Monday in quiet trade as most market participants were wary of making fresh purchases ahead of the end of the current fiscal year March 31.  \par \par \uc2 India's fiscal year runs through April to March.  \par \par \uc2 Quarterly corporate tax payments beginning Tuesday also raised near-term liquidity concerns, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Friday's rally in global oil prices and U.S Treasury yields also weighed on bonds.  \par \par \uc2 The actively traded 7.38% paper, due 2015, ended at INR106.05, down from INR106.11 at the previous close and Monday's high of INR106.14.  \par \par \uc2 The yield on the paper rose to 6.57% from 6.56% Saturday.  \par \par \uc2 "Most people believe that there will not be any buying support as everyone is buying very selectively before the financial year end," said a dealer at a state-run bank.  \par \par \uc2 Market participants are wary of building large positions ahead of the fiscal year-end due to liquidity concerns.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier, bond prices rose marginally as some state-run banks and an insurance company bought bonds selectively.  \par \par \uc2 However, prices soon started sliding as demand ebbed, said dealers.  \par \par \uc2 "There are absolutely no fresh factors to guide the market," said a dealer at a private sector bank.  \par \par \uc2 "Any significant price changes will take place only after the federal government announces the schedule for its borrowing program next year," he added.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said any sharp changes in global oil prices also could act as a trigger.  \par \par \uc2 Bonds may remain in a tight range for next two weeks due to lower demand, as major buyers of government bonds are unlikely to buy large volumes ahead of the fiscal year end, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 The overnight call money rate ended steady Monday at 4.25%-4.50% in ample system liquidity.  \par \par \uc2 The rupee ended marginally higher against the dollar, but off the day's highs as state-run banks bought dollars in the late trade.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar ended at INR43.5550 Monday, down from INR43.5650 Friday, but off the day's low of INR43.5000.  \par \par \uc2 "The rupee started on a strong note, but began falling after state-run banks started buying (dollars)," said a dealer at foreign bank.  \par \par \uc2 State-run banks have been major buyers of dollars in the spot market in recent sessions, possibly on behalf of the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  often \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to curb the overvaluation of the rupee in trade terms, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier, the rupee had risen on strong dollar sales by foreign banks, possibly for foreign portfolio investors, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Foreign funds invested more than $1.27 billion in the local stock market in March, compared with around $1.9 billion in February.  \par \par \uc2 "The rupee could have breached 43.50 level if the state-run banks had not started buying dollars," said dealer at a private sector bank.  \par \par \uc2 Dollar forward rates ended mostly steady Monday, with the forward dollar contract maturing in February 2006 ending at a premium of INR0.535 to the spot rate.  \par \par \uc2 MIFOR-based interest rate swaps ended up on corporate buying. The five-year swap rose to 5.90%-5.95% from 5.82%-5.84% at the previous close, while two-year swap hardened to 5.25%-5.30% from 5.20%-5.24%.  \par \par \uc2 Overnight indexed swaps ended slightly higher in line with government bond yields. The five-year swap closed at 6.36%-6.39%, compared with 6.35%-6.38% Friday. [ 14-03-05 1321GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Rupee Vs Dollar Close \par Monday          Previous \par INR43.5550      INR43.5650 \par  \par Forward Dollar Premiums/Discounts \par May                    August                 February \par INR0.1400 Vs INR0.1375 INR0.2900 Vs INR0.2925 INR0.535 Vs INR0.535 \par  \par Daily Overnight Reverse Repurchase Auction Amount \par Monday          Previous \par INR343.30 Bln   INR336.10 Bln \par  \par Reverse Repurchase Auction Rate \par Monday          Previous \par 4.75%           4.75% \par  \par Call Rate \par Monday          Previous Close \par 4.25%-4.50%     4.25%-4.50% \par  \par Interest Rate Swaps  Current Price   Previous Price \par  2-Year Mifor swap   5.25%-5.30%     5.20%-5.24% \par  5-Year Mifor swap   5.90%-5.95%     5.82%-5.84% \par  5-Year OIS          6.36%-6.39%     6.35%-6.38% \par  \par Benchmark Bond Prices \par Security   Maturity   Current Price   Previous Price \par  7.55% GOI   2010        105.10          105.17 \par  7.38% GOI   2015        106.05          106.11 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Madhu T, Dow Jones Newswires; 91 22 22884212; madhu.nair@dowjones.com [ 14-03-05 1321GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1164 1847 62506  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050314e13e000np\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
