\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee gives up gains under cbank pressure.  \b0\par\par\uc2 254 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2005\par 12:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BOMBAY, March 7 (Reuters) - The rupee gave up most of the\par day's gains to end almost unchanged on Monday, as a watchful\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  continued its dollar buying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to keep\par appreciation in the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from hurting Indian exports.\par     The rupee          closed at 43.7300/7350 per dollar, 0.06\par percent stronger than Friday's 43.75/76 close, but well off its\par session peak of 43.6750.\par     "It was the same old story," a dealer at a state-run bank\par said. "The moment it started heading towards 43.65, they\par (state-run banks that buy on behalf of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) came\par in and thereafter it was just stuck in a very narrow band."\par     Foreign funds have been flowing into India's stock market,\par with net purchases so far this year at more than a quarter of\par the record $8.5 billion overseas investors bought in 2004.\par     The Reserve Bank of India has been absorbing a bulk of the\par inflows since early February, after they propelled the rupee to\par a five-year peak of 43.30.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has swelled \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par reserves to a record $135.66 billion, an increase of $5 billion\par over two weeks.\par     Traders said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was likely to remain in a tight\par range on Tuesday and volumes would shrink as banks in the\par financial capital Bombay will be closed for a local holiday.\par  \par -------------------------------------------------------------\par                                   MARKET SNAPSHOT\par     Bombay Sensitive Index\par     Indian rupee (/$)\par     7.38 percent 2015 bond (%)\par -------------------------------------------------------------.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050307e1370087t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
