\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 India \highlight\uc2  rupee gives up gain, cbank  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  seen.  \b0\par\par\uc2 353 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2005\par 13:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BOMBAY, March 3 (Reuters) - The rupee was supported for\par most of Thursday on demand from banks who trade on behalf of\par foreign funds, but gave up its gains in late trading as dealers\par said the Reserve Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  again \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to limit its\par appreciation.\par     "Towards the end of the day, the RBI came in again.\par Otherwise it should have firmed," said one dealer at a\par state-run bank.\par     The rupee          closed at 43.7000/7100 per dollar,\par unchanged on the day but off the day's 43.6350 peak it hit on\par strong foreign capital inflows as Bombay's soaring share index\par climbed to a new closing high.\par     India's benchmark share index          rose 1.5 percent to\par a record 6,784.72 points, driven by increased interest from\par abroad following a pro-growth federal budget presented by the\par left-leaning government this week.\par     Dealers said the rupee's gains past the 43.65-per-dollar\par level seemed to trigger dollar-buying by state-run banks, which\par usually operate on behalf of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     "It was a question of when they would come in," said a\par trader at a state-run bank.\par     Bankers say the recently concluded initial public offering\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 of India's biggest domestic airline, Jet Airways (India) Ltd.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 , would draw in about $150 million worth of foreign  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 inflows by next Thursday.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     Foreign funds invested more than $2 billion in the stock\par market in the first two months of 2005.\par     The Reserve Bank of India has been buying dollars steadily\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 since mid-February. The Indian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  set a five-year high of  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 43.30 in early February, but has drifted lower on sustained  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and is down about 1 percent.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts say the RBI is keen to check the rupee, which is  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 allowed to float in an unofficial target band of 3-5 percent  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 against the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of India's top-five trading partners.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     JP Morgan estimates the rupee is overvalued by about 3\par percent on a trade-weighted basis .\par -------------------------------------------------------------\par                                   MARKET SNAPSHOT\par     Bombay Sensitive Index\par     Indian rupee (/$)\par     7.38 percent 2015 bond (%)\par -------------------------------------------------------------.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050303e133009ug\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
