\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee ends tad firmer amid cbank wariness.  \b0\par\par\uc2 299 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2005\par 12:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BOMBAY, March 2 (Reuters) - The rupee ended slightly firmer\par on Wednesday, supported by strong inflows of foreign capital\par but reined in after sustained \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Reserve Bank\par of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to maintain export competitiveness, dealers said.\par     The Indian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2           closed at 43.70/71 per dollar,\par up 0.06 percent from the previous close. It traded in a tight\par range of 43.70-74, amid talk that state-run banks that often\par act on behalf of the RBI were again buying dollars on\par Wednesday.\par     "The rupee is very well protected by the RBI," said one\par trader at a state-run bank. "The indication is that there is\par not much scope for the rupee to gain," he said, adding that the\par 43.50 level would probably cap any rally in the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     The firmer dollar, which set a one-week peak against the\par euro  on Wednesday, was also helping limit the rupee's\par gains, dealers said.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The Indian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  set a five-year high of 43.30 per\par dollar in early February, but has since drifted lower on\par sustained \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Analysts say the RBI is\par keen to check the rupee, overvalued by 3 percent on a\par trade-weighted basis.\par     Rallying shares drew in record foreign fund inflows of $8.5\par billion in 2004, lifting the rupee 5 percent last year and\par strong inflows of foreign capital into booming Bombay stocks\par continued to underpin the rupee.\par     Foreign funds, which invested $2 billion in local shares in\par the first two months of 2005, snapped up about 8.6 million\par shares in India's most valuable company, Oil and Natural Gas\par Corp. (ONGC.BO) for about $170 million on Wednesday.\par  \par -------------------------------------------------------------\par                                   MARKET SNAPSHOT\par     Bombay Sensitive Index\par     Indian rupee (/$)\par     7.38 percent 2015 bond (%)\par -------------------------------------------------------------.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050302e1320093z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
