\par\par \b \uc2 Indian bonds seen lower as oil surges.   \b0\par\par\uc2 324 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Februar 2005\par 04:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BOMBAY, Feb 23 (Reuters) - Indian federal bonds could ease\par on Wednesday, with a jump in U.S. oil futures to above $51 a\par barrel raising concerns of inflationary pressures, traders\par said.\par     But losses will be limited due to ample cash, a result of\par the Reserve Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to rein in the rupee, they said.\par     NYMEX crude for April delivery        was at $51.29 a\par barrel, down just 13 cents in Asian trade after the expiring\par March contract jumped $2.80 a barrel on Tuesday, touching a\par 16-week high $51.40.\par     India imports about 70 percent of its oil needs, and a\par spurt in prices last year lifted inflation to a 3-1/2-year high\par of 8.74 percent in August, prompting the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to raise\par interest rates for the first time in more than four years.\par     Inflation has since slowed to 5.01 percent.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Traders will want to await next Monday's annual budget,\par which will announce the government's borrowing programme for\par the fiscal year beginning on April 1.\par     A Reuters poll of 10 fixed-income analysts showed a median\par forecast for net borrowings for 2005/06 of 940 billion rupees,\par 4 percent up from the 904 billion in the previous budget drawn\par up in July after a change in government.\par  \par     On Tuesday, the yield on the benchmark 10-year bond\par   ended little changed at 6.5053 percent.\par     Dealers will also be watching to see if the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 succeeds in raising the target of 63 billion rupees for states   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 as the on-tap sale of 7.17 percent 12-year loans for 10   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 remaining state governments enters the second day.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  closed on Tuesday the sale of loans for   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Andhra Pradesh, Himachal Pradesh, Jammu and Kashmir, Kerala,   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Orissa and Punjab.    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par -------------------------------------------------------------\par                           MARKET SNAPSHOT\par      Bombay Sensitive Index\par      Indian rupee (/$)\par      7.38 percent 2015 bond (%)\par -------------------------------------------------------------.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050223e12n0025t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
