\par\par \b \uc2 Indian Govt Bonds Up On Bargain Hunting; Rupee Up  \b0\par\par\uc2 763 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Februar 2005\par 13:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--Indian government bond prices ended higher Monday on bargain hunting after losses in the recent sessions.  \par \par \uc2 However, the prices ended below the days high as some banks sold bonds to raise cash for investing in the state government bond sale Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 Sixteen of India's 29 state governments plan to sell bonds worth a total INR63 billion Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 The actively traded 7.38% paper, due 2015, ended at INR106.59, up from INR106.50 Saturday, but below the day's high of INR106.62. Its yield fell to 6.51% from 6.52%.  \par \par \uc2 "Banks were buying steadily, but they were a bit cautious because of tomorrow's state government bond sale," said a dealer at a state-run bank.  \par \par \uc2 The federal budget, due Feb. 28, also weighed on market sentiment.  \par \par \uc2 "You won't see any sharp movement in government bonds in the near term. Most players would wait for the budget before making big purchases," said a dealer at a private sector bank.  \par \par \uc2 Bonds are expected to remain mostly in a narrow range Tuesday morning as market participants are likely to wait for the outcome of the state bond sale.  \par \par \uc2 The overnight call money rate Monday closed slightly higher at 4.70%-4.80%, compared with 4.60-4.80% Saturday, amid ample money market liquidity.  \par \par \uc2 The rupee ended higher against the dollar Monday as state-run banks, the main buyers of the U.S. unit in past two weeks, stayed away from the market.  \par \par \uc2 However, the rupee fell back from the day's highs on scattered bargain hunting for the U.S \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially in late trade, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar ended at INR43.8150, down from INR43.8450 previous close, but above the day's low of INR43.7900.  \par \par \uc2 "Players are treading very cautiously. Nobody wants to build a large position because everyone is convinced that state-run banks may start buying the dollar again," said a dealer at a foreign bank.  \par \par \uc2 State-run banks have been buying dollars consistently in the past two weeks, possibly on behalf of the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The RBI often \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market through state-run banks to curb the appreciation of the rupee to maintain the export competitiveness of Indian companies.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market as the rupee is still considered overvalued by more than 3% in trade-related terms, said analysts.  \par \par \uc2 "If you look at the pattern of state-run banking, you may find that they always start buying around 43.75-43.77 levels. It means they will not let rupee appreciate beyond this level," said a dealer at a foreign bank.  \par \par \uc2 However, without the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the rupee may appreciate against the dollar as fundamentals support a stronger rupee, said dealers.  \par \par \uc2 Dollar forward rates ended mostly steady Friday as the spot market remained in a tight range. The forward dollar contract maturing, in January 2006, ended unchanged at a premium of INR0.595 to the spot rate.  \par \par \uc2 MIFOR-based interest rate swaps ended marginally up on higher U.S treasury yields. The five-year swap ended at 5.53%-5.58%, compared with 5.48%-5.52% at the previous close, while the two-year swap rose to 5.03%-5.07%, compared with 4.99%-5.04%.  \par \par \uc2 Overnight indexed swaps ended steady, with the five-year swap closing at 6.25%-6.27%, compared with 6.24%-6.27% Friday.  \par \par \uc2 -By Madhu T, Dow Jones Newswires; 91 22 22884212; madhu.nair@dowjones.com [ 21-02-05 1249GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Rupee Vs Dollar Close \par Monday          Previous \par INR43.8150      INR43.8450 \par  \par Forward Dollar Premiums/Discounts \par April                 July                   January \par INR0.15 Vs INR0.15    INR0.33 Vs INR0.325    INR0.605 Vs INR0.605 \par  \par Daily Overnight Reverse Repurchase Auction Amount \par Monday          Previous \par INR136.95 Bln   INR259.65 Bln \par  \par Reverse Repurchase Auction Rate \par Monday          Previous \par 4.75%           4.75% \par  \par Call Rate \par Monday          Previous Close \par 4.70%-4.80%     4.60%-4.80% \par  \par Interest Rate Swaps  Current Price   Previous Price \par  2-Year Mifor swap   5.03%-5.07%     4.99%-5.04% \par  5-Year Mifor swap   5.53%-5.58%     5.48%-5.52% \par  5-Year OIS          6.25%-6.27%     6.24%-6.27% \par  \par Benchmark Bond Prices \par Security   Maturity   Current Price   Previous Price \par  7.38% GOI   2015        106.59          106.50 \par  7.55% GOI   2010        105.36          105.30   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Madhu T, Dow Jones Newswires; 91 22 22884212; madhu.nair@dowjones.com [ 21-02-05 1250GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1164 1847 62506  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050221e12l000kc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
