\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee firms tentatively, mkt wary of cbank.  \b0\par\par\uc2 361 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Februar 2005\par 05:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BOMBAY, Feb 11 (Reuters) - The rupee firmed at the start of\par trade on Friday after data showing a record U.S. trade deficit\par halted the dollar's six-week rally against major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ,\par but traders remained cautious about possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par activity.\par     Sentiment continued to be upbeat about the rupee, which hit\par five-year highs at 43.30 per dollar last week, but traders were\par eyeing the Reserve Bank of India after state-run banks,\par possibly acting for the RBI, bought dollars for a third day on\par Thursday.\par     "It's a little quiet and tentative after the last three\par days of RBI-led nationalised bank buying (of dollars)," said a\par trader at a foreign bank, adding that the state-run banks had\par actively bought dollars around the 43.70 level on Thursday.\par     At 9:40 a.m. (0410 GMT), the rupee          was at\par 43.7250/7350 per dollar, 0.1 percent firmer that Thursday's\par close at 43.77/78 -- its weakest close since Jan.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  27\par     The euro        and the yen        edged up against the\par dollar, which was plagued by renewed concern about a record\par U.S. trade deficit. Data showed the deficit had surged to a\par record $617.7 billion in 2004.\par     The RBI \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  through state-run banks to curb\par excessive volatility in the market and to maintain the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's competitiveness but rarely comments on its\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     "If the Reserve Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would be buying (dollars),\par they could buy up to 43.81," said a trader with a state-run\par bank.\par     "If the RBI doesn't come in, it could come down to 43.65."\par     The rupee is still overvalued by about 4 percent on a\par trade-weighted basis, analysts said, adding this could have\par sparked off the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     The RBI uses a monitoring band for guiding the rupee in the\par medium term, which allows it to float within a 3 to 5 percent\par range determined by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of India's top five trading\par partners, but it publishes the data with a lag.\par  \par  -------------------------------------------------------------\par                                   MARKET SNAPSHOT\par     Bombay Sensitive Index\par     Indian rupee (/$)\par     7.38 percent 2015 bond (%)\par  -------------------------------------------------------------.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050211e12b004eh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
