\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee inches up early as dollar pauses.  \b0\par\par\uc2 324 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Februar 2005\par 05:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BOMBAY, Feb 10 (Reuters) - The rupee inched up on Thursday\par morning as the dollar's recent rally against the major\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  lost some steam overseas, but traders were wary of\par further rupee gains amid expectations of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     At 9:15 a.m. (0345 GMT), the Indian rupee          was\par quoted at 43.6950/7050 per dollar, up from the previous close\par of 43.7350/7450 which was the weakest since Jan. 27.\par     "The rupee has risen on the back of the dollar's drop\par overseas," said a chief dealer at a private sector bank. "But\par there are expectations the state-run banks will come to buy at\par 43.70 per dollar levels. So, sentiment is a bit edgy and\par traders will not go short on the dollar."\par     The rupee, which is partially convertible, has lost 0.5\par percent since the start of the week as the dollar gained\par overseas and after state-run banks bought dollars, amid growing\par talk they could be acting on behalf of the Reserve Bank of\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (RBI).\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The RBI \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  through state-run banks to curb\par excessive volatility in the market and to maintain the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's export competitiveness.\par     The dollar hung near U.S. levels in Asian trade as it lost\par steam after hitting three-month highs against the euro\par and a two-month high versus the yen  earlier this week.\par     Still, JP Morgan Chase's real effective exchange rate index\par   showed the rupee was overvalued by 4 percent on\par Wednesday, which analysts said could have sparked off the\par latest bout of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     The RBI uses a monitoring band for the rupee, which allows\par it to float within a 3 to 5 percent range determined by the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of India's top five trading partners, but it\par publishes the data with a lag.\par  \par  -------------------------------------------------------------\par                                   MARKET SNAPSHOT\par     Bombay Sensitive Index\par     Indian rupee (/$)\par     7.38 percent 2015 bond (%)\par  -------------------------------------------------------------.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050210e12a0043d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
